// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 6): technology and space.
ADDART({id:"dishwashers",t:"Dishwashers",c:"Technology",i:"🍽️",w:"Dishwasher",r:["washingmachine","vacuumcleaner","feminism","elec","waterscarcity"],
p:[["Machines for dishes","Dishwashers clean plates, cups and cutlery with hot water sprays."],["Invented by a socialite","Josephine Cochrane patented a practical dishwasher in 1886."],["Saving water","Modern dishwashers often use less water than washing by hand."]],
l:"A dishwasher is a machine that cleans dishes, glasses, cutlery and cookware automatically. Dishes are loaded into racks, and the machine sprays them with hot water and detergent from rotating arms, then rinses and dries them. Early hand-cranked designs appeared in the 1850s, but the first practical dishwasher was invented by Josephine Cochrane, a wealthy woman from Illinois who wanted a machine that would wash dishes faster than her servants and without chipping her fine china. She built a machine in which dishes sat in wire compartments inside a wheel while water was sprayed onto them, patented it in 1886 and showed it at the 1893 World’s Columbian Exposition in Chicago, where it won an award. Her company later became part of KitchenAid. Dishwashers were used mainly in hotels and restaurants until after World War II, when smaller home models spread. Studies have found that modern efficient dishwashers usually use less water and energy than washing the same dishes by hand under running water.",
s:[["How it works","The machine heats water, often to about 50–70°C, and pumps it through spinning spray arms. Detergents contain enzymes that break down proteins and starches. A filter catches food scraps, and the dirty water is pumped out. Some models dry dishes with heat; others use the residual heat and condensation."],["Josephine Cochrane","Cochrane, a descendant of the inventor of an early steamboat, worked in a shed behind her house with a mechanic to build her machine. After her husband died, leaving debts, she turned the invention into a business, selling it first to hotels and restaurants."],["Water and energy","A modern dishwasher may use about 10–15 liters of water per cycle, while washing a full load by hand under a running tap can use several times as much. Eco programs use lower temperatures for longer times. Scraping dishes instead of pre-rinsing them saves more water."],["Around the world","Dishwashers are common in North America and Western Europe but much less so in many other countries, where kitchens are smaller and hand-washing is traditional. In Japan and China, compact countertop dishwashers have become more popular."]],
k:[["Practical inventor","Josephine Cochrane"],["Patent","1886"],["Award","1893 Chicago World’s Fair"],["Water per cycle","About 10–15 liters (modern)"],["Main parts","Racks, spray arms, pump, filter"]],
d:[["1850","Joel Houghton patents a hand-turned dish-washing device"],["1886","Josephine Cochrane patents her dishwasher"],["1893","Cochrane’s machine wins an award in Chicago"],["1949","KitchenAid sells a home dishwasher"],["1970s","Dishwashers become common in American homes"]],
s2:[["What not to put in","Wooden utensils, cast-iron pans, sharp knives and some plastics can be damaged by the heat and detergents. Hand-painted china and crystal may fade or cloud over many washes."],["Inventors’ Hall of Fame","Josephine Cochrane was inducted into the US National Inventors Hall of Fame in 2006, recognizing one of the most important household inventions by a woman."]],
sp:["Cooking in the dishwasher","Some cooks have experimented with ‘dishwasher cooking’, sealing salmon or vegetables in jars or bags and cooking them gently in the hot water of a wash cycle, a novelty that works like low-temperature sous-vide cooking."],
g:[["Detergent","A cleaning chemical"],["Enzyme","A protein that speeds up chemical reactions"],["Cycle","One complete run of a machine"],["Condensation","Water vapor turning into liquid"]],
f:["Josephine Cochrane invented the practical dishwasher.","Modern dishwashers often save water.","Cochrane’s company became part of KitchenAid."],
fun:"Josephine Cochrane reportedly said, ‘If nobody else is going to invent a dishwashing machine, I’ll do it myself.’",
tl:"Naglilinis ang dishwasher ng mga plato, tasa at kubyertos gamit ang wisik ng mainit na tubig. Inimbento ni Josephine Cochrane ang praktikal na dishwasher noong 1886, at kadalasang mas kaunting tubig ang gamit nito kaysa sa paghuhugas sa kamay."});

ADDART({id:"microphones",t:"Microphones",c:"Technology",i:"🎤",w:"Microphone",r:["sound","headphones","radio","phone","smartphone"],
p:[["Capturing sound","Microphones turn sound waves into electrical signals."],["From telephones to studios","Microphones developed with the telephone and radio."],["Tiny chips","Smartphones use microphones on microchips."]],
l:"A microphone is a device that converts sound waves into electrical signals that can be amplified, recorded or transmitted. Sound waves make a thin diaphragm vibrate, and the microphone turns those vibrations into a changing electric current. Microphones were developed along with the telephone: Alexander Graham Bell used a liquid transmitter in 1876, and in 1877–1878 Emile Berliner, Thomas Edison and David Edward Hughes developed carbon microphones, in which sound pressed carbon granules together, changing their resistance; carbon microphones were used in telephones for about a century. The condenser microphone, invented by E. C. Wente at Bell Labs in 1916, and the dynamic and ribbon microphones of the 1920s and 1930s made high-quality radio broadcasting and recording possible. Today billions of tiny MEMS microphones, built on silicon chips, are used in phones, laptops, hearing aids and smart speakers. Different microphones pick up sound from different directions, and singers, broadcasters and filmmakers choose them for particular uses.",
s:[["Types","Dynamic microphones use a coil attached to a diaphragm moving in a magnetic field, like a loudspeaker in reverse; they are tough and common on stage. Condenser microphones use a diaphragm forming one plate of a capacitor and need power; they are sensitive and used in studios. Ribbon microphones use a thin metal ribbon and give a warm sound."],["Directional patterns","Omnidirectional microphones pick up sound from all directions. Cardioid microphones, named after their heart-shaped pickup pattern, mainly capture sound from the front, reducing noise from the audience. Shotgun microphones, used in film, pick up sound from a narrow direction at a distance."],["Microphones and music","Microphones changed singing. Before them, singers had to project loudly to fill halls. From the late 1920s, ‘crooners’ such as Bing Crosby sang softly and intimately into microphones, and the electric microphone made modern popular music possible."],["MEMS microphones","Micro-electromechanical systems (MEMS) microphones are a few millimeters across and are made like computer chips. A modern smartphone may contain three or more, used together to cancel background noise and detect the direction of a voice."]],
k:[["Converts","Sound to electrical signals"],["Carbon microphone","1877–1878"],["Condenser microphone","E. C. Wente, 1916"],["Stage favorite","Dynamic microphone"],["In phones","MEMS microphones"]],
d:[["1876","Bell’s liquid transmitter carries speech"],["1878","Carbon microphones developed"],["1916","Condenser microphone invented"],["1931","Ribbon microphone developed by RCA"],["2000s","MEMS microphones spread into phones"]],
s2:[["The word","The word ‘microphone’ was used in the 1820s for an acoustic device to amplify faint sounds. David Edward Hughes applied it to his electrical carbon device in 1878, showing that it could make the footsteps of a fly audible."],["Mic drop","Dropping a microphone at the end of a performance, the ‘mic drop’, became a popular gesture of triumph. Sound engineers dislike it because it can damage microphones and speakers."]],
sp:["Feedback","The loud squeal heard at concerts and speeches is acoustic feedback, caused when a microphone picks up sound from a nearby loudspeaker, which is amplified again in a loop. Positioning microphones behind speakers prevents it."],
g:[["Diaphragm","A thin sheet that vibrates with sound"],["Capacitor","A device that stores electric charge"],["Cardioid","A heart-shaped pickup pattern"],["MEMS","Tiny mechanical devices built on chips"]],
f:["Carbon microphones were used in telephones for about a century.","Phones use tiny microphones built like chips.","A dynamic microphone is like a loudspeaker in reverse."],
fun:"David Edward Hughes demonstrated that his early microphone could make the sound of a fly walking on a box audible.",
tl:"Ginagawang senyas na elektrikal ng mikropono ang mga alon ng tunog. Nabuo ito kasabay ng telepono at radyo, at gumagamit ang mga smartphone ng napakaliit na mikropono sa microchip."});

ADDART({id:"smokedetectors",t:"Smoke Detectors",c:"Technology",i:"🚨",w:"Smoke detector",r:["firefighters","radioact","elec","smarthome","sound"],
p:[["Early warning","Smoke detectors sound an alarm when they sense smoke."],["Saving lives","They greatly reduce deaths in house fires."],["Two main types","Ionization and photoelectric detectors sense smoke differently."]],
l:"A smoke detector, or smoke alarm, is a device that senses smoke and sounds a loud alarm to warn people of a fire. Most home fire deaths happen at night, when people are asleep and may not smell smoke, so smoke alarms give crucial extra minutes to escape. Early smoke detectors were developed in the 1930s by Swiss physicist Walter Jaeger, who discovered by accident that cigarette smoke affected an ionization sensor he was building to detect poison gas. Affordable battery-powered home smoke alarms appeared in the United States in the late 1960s and 1970s, and many countries now require them by law in homes. There are two main types: ionization alarms, which use a tiny amount of radioactive americium-241 to detect fast-flaming fires, and photoelectric alarms, which use a light beam and react faster to slow, smoldering fires. Studies by the US National Fire Protection Association show that the risk of dying in a home fire is about 60 percent lower in homes with working smoke alarms. Alarms must be tested regularly and replaced about every ten years.",
s:[["Ionization alarms","Inside an ionization chamber, a tiny source of americium-241 ionizes the air, letting a small current flow between two plates. Smoke particles disrupt the current, triggering the alarm. The amount of radioactive material is very small and safe in normal use."],["Photoelectric alarms","In a photoelectric alarm, a light source shines away from a sensor. When smoke enters, particles scatter light onto the sensor, setting off the alarm. Photoelectric alarms are less prone to false alarms from cooking and respond faster to smoldering fires such as those started by cigarettes."],["Keeping alarms working","Many fire deaths occur in homes where alarms have dead or removed batteries, often taken out because of nuisance alarms. Sealed alarms with ten-year batteries, interconnected alarms that all sound together and alarms with strobe lights or bed shakers for deaf people improve safety."],["Carbon monoxide alarms","Carbon monoxide, produced by faulty heaters and boilers, is a colorless, odorless poison gas. Separate or combined carbon monoxide alarms detect it and are required in many homes with fuel-burning appliances."]],
k:[["Main types","Ionization and photoelectric"],["Ionization source","Americium-241"],["Early inventor","Walter Jaeger (1930s)"],["Death risk with working alarms","About 60% lower (NFPA)"],["Replace alarms","About every 10 years"]],
d:[["1890","Early electric fire alarm patented in the US"],["1930s","Walter Jaeger discovers smoke sets off an ionization sensor"],["1965","Battery-powered home smoke alarm patented"],["1970s","Home smoke alarms become widespread in the US"],["1990s","Many countries require smoke alarms in new homes"]],
s2:[["The accidental discovery","Walter Jaeger was trying to build a sensor for poison gas. Frustrated when it did not work, he lit a cigarette, and the sensor responded to the smoke, leading to the first ionization smoke detectors used in industry."],["Test once a month","Fire services recommend pressing the test button monthly and having an alarm on every level of a home, outside sleeping areas. Many recommend changing clocks and checking alarms at the same time."]],
sp:["The chirp","A smoke alarm that chirps every minute or so is usually warning that its battery is low. Many chirps happen at night because cooling temperatures reduce battery voltage."],
g:[["Ionization","Giving atoms an electric charge"],["Photoelectric","Using light to detect something"],["Americium-241","A radioactive element used in smoke alarms"],["Carbon monoxide","A poisonous, odorless gas"]],
f:["Working smoke alarms cut the risk of dying in a fire by about 60%.","Ionization alarms contain a tiny radioactive source.","Smoke alarms should be replaced about every ten years."],
fun:"The first smoke detector was discovered by accident when an inventor lit a cigarette near a gas sensor that was not working.",
tl:"Tumutunog ang smoke detector kapag nakadarama ito ng usok. Malaki ang ibinababa nito sa bilang ng namamatay sa sunog sa bahay, at may dalawang pangunahing uri: ionization at photoelectric."});

ADDART({id:"atms",t:"ATMs",c:"Technology",i:"🏧",w:"Automated teller machine",r:["money","digitalpay","cybersecurity","passwords","cryptocurrency"],
p:[["Cash machines","ATMs let people withdraw cash and check accounts without a bank teller."],["First in London","The first cash machine opened in London in 1967."],["Fewer ATMs","As digital payments grow, the number of ATMs is falling in many countries."]],
l:"An automated teller machine (ATM), also called a cash machine or cashpoint, is an electronic device that lets bank customers withdraw cash, check balances and sometimes deposit money or pay bills without visiting a bank teller. Users insert a bank card or tap a phone and enter a personal identification number (PIN). The first cash machine was installed by Barclays bank in Enfield, north London, on 27 June 1967, designed by a team led by John Shepherd-Barron; it used paper vouchers treated with radioactive carbon-14 rather than plastic cards. Other machines appeared in Sweden, Japan and the United States around the same time, and ATMs linked to bank computers spread worldwide in the 1970s and 1980s, letting people get cash at any hour. There are roughly 3 million ATMs worldwide. As card payments, mobile wallets and instant transfers grow, ATM numbers are falling in many countries, raising concerns that people who rely on cash, often older, poorer or rural people, could be left behind. Criminals target ATMs with card skimmers, malware and physical attacks.",
s:[["The first cash machine","Shepherd-Barron said he got the idea in his bath after arriving at his bank too late to withdraw money. The first machine’s first customer was the comedy actor Reg Varney. He chose a four-digit PIN because his wife could remember only four numbers."],["How ATMs work","ATMs connect to bank networks that check whether the card is valid and whether the account has enough money. Machines contain locked cassettes of banknotes and count notes using sensors. Networks such as Visa and Mastercard let people use ATMs of other banks and in other countries."],["Security","Skimmers are hidden devices attached to card slots to copy card details, while tiny cameras record PINs. Banks have added anti-skimming hardware and chip cards. Criminals have also used ‘jackpotting’ malware to make machines spit out cash, and explosive attacks led banks to install ink-staining systems."],["A cashless future?","In Sweden, cash use has fallen sharply, and many bank branches no longer handle cash. The United Kingdom and other countries have introduced rules to protect access to cash, requiring banks to provide cash services in communities."]],
k:[["First cash machine","Barclays, Enfield, London, 27 June 1967"],["Inventor credited","John Shepherd-Barron"],["Security code","PIN"],["Number worldwide","About 3 million"],["Trend","Falling in many countries"]],
d:[["1967","First cash machine opens in London"],["1969","First ATM in the United States, in New York"],["1970s","ATMs linked to bank networks"],["1990s","Chip-and-PIN cards developed"],["2010s","ATM numbers begin to fall in many countries"]],
s2:[["A gold ATM","Some ATMs dispense gold bars and coins, such as machines introduced in Dubai and Germany in 2010. Bitcoin ATMs, which exchange cash for cryptocurrency, have also spread, though authorities warn they are often used in scams."],["The Vatican’s Latin ATM","An ATM in Vatican City offers instructions in Latin, among other languages, one of the few machines in the world to do so."]],
sp:["Radioactive vouchers","Customers using the first London cash machine inserted a paper voucher marked with carbon-14. The machine detected the radioactivity, matched it with a PIN and paid out a fixed amount of 10 pounds."],
g:[["PIN","Personal identification number"],["Skimmer","A device that secretly copies card data"],["Teller","A bank employee who handles cash"],["Cashless","Using no physical money"]],
f:["The first cash machine opened in London in 1967.","The first PIN was four digits because of a wife’s memory.","ATM numbers are falling in many countries."],
fun:"The first person to use a cash machine was a television comedy actor, Reg Varney, in 1967.",
tl:"Pinapayagan ng ATM ang mga tao na kumuha ng pera at tingnan ang account nang walang teller ng bangko. Binuksan ang unang cash machine sa London noong 1967, at bumababa na ang bilang ng ATM sa maraming bansa."});

ADDART({id:"heatpumps",t:"Heat Pumps",c:"Technology",i:"♨️",w:"Heat pump",r:["refrigeration","thermo","energytransition","elec","climchange"],
p:[["Moving heat","Heat pumps move heat from outside air, ground or water into buildings."],["Very efficient","They can deliver three or more units of heat for each unit of electricity."],["Cutting emissions","Heat pumps are key to replacing gas and oil boilers."]],
l:"A heat pump is a machine that moves heat from one place to another using electricity, rather than producing heat by burning fuel. It works like a refrigerator in reverse: a refrigerant fluid absorbs heat from the outside air, the ground or water, even when they are cold, is compressed to raise its temperature, and releases the heat indoors; many heat pumps can also run backward to cool buildings in summer. Because they move heat rather than create it, heat pumps are very efficient, typically delivering three to four units of heat for every unit of electricity they use. Heat pumps are central to plans to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from heating, which accounts for a large share of energy use in cold countries, especially when powered by low-carbon electricity. Norway, Sweden and Finland use heat pumps in a large share of homes, and they are common in Japan and China. According to the International Energy Agency, global sales fell slightly in 2025, but European sales rose 11 percent after two years of decline, led by Germany.",
s:[["How it works","A heat pump has four main parts: an evaporator, where cold refrigerant absorbs heat; a compressor, which squeezes it and makes it hot; a condenser, where it releases heat into the home; and an expansion valve, which lowers its pressure so the cycle can repeat. Air-source heat pumps take heat from outside air; ground-source pumps use pipes buried in the ground."],["Efficiency","Efficiency is measured by the coefficient of performance (COP): a COP of 3 means 3 kilowatt-hours of heat for each kilowatt-hour of electricity. Efficiency falls in very cold weather, but modern air-source heat pumps work at temperatures below −20°C, as shown by their popularity in Norway."],["History","The principle was described by Lord Kelvin in 1852. Austrian engineer Peter von Rittinger built an early heat pump in 1856 to evaporate salt water. The first large heat pump to heat a building was installed in Zurich’s city hall in 1938, using water from the Limmat River."],["Challenges","Heat pumps cost more to buy and install than gas boilers, and they work best in well-insulated homes. Governments offer grants to encourage them. Some older refrigerants are powerful greenhouse gases, so manufacturers are switching to alternatives such as propane and carbon dioxide."]],
k:[["Works by","Moving heat, not burning fuel"],["Typical efficiency","3–4 units of heat per unit of electricity"],["Main types","Air-source and ground-source"],["First building heat pump","Zurich City Hall, 1938"],["2025 sales","Global −2%; Europe +11% (IEA)"]],
d:[["1852","Lord Kelvin describes the heat pump principle"],["1856","Peter von Rittinger builds an early heat pump"],["1938","Heat pump installed in Zurich City Hall"],["1970s","Oil crises boost interest in heat pumps"],["2022","Sales surge in Europe after gas prices spike"],["2025","European heat pump sales rise again"]],
s2:[["Norway","Norway has one of the highest numbers of heat pumps per household in the world, with most homes using one. Cheap hydroelectric power and cold winters make heat pumps an obvious choice there."],["Heat pumps in your kitchen","A refrigerator is a heat pump: it pumps heat out of the food compartment and releases it from coils at the back, which is why the back of a fridge feels warm."]],
sp:["Heat from rivers and sewers","Large heat pumps can warm whole districts using heat from rivers, the sea, sewage or data centers. Helsinki and Stockholm use heat pumps to recover heat from treated wastewater."],
g:[["Refrigerant","A fluid that absorbs and releases heat"],["Compressor","A device that squeezes gas"],["Coefficient of performance","A measure of heat pump efficiency"],["Ground-source","Taking heat from the ground"]],
f:["Heat pumps can deliver three to four times more heat than the electricity they use.","A refrigerator is a kind of heat pump.","European heat pump sales rose 11% in 2025."],
fun:"Even air at −10°C contains plenty of heat energy, which a heat pump can extract and concentrate to warm a house.",
tl:"Inililipat ng heat pump ang init mula sa hangin, lupa o tubig sa labas papasok sa mga gusali. Napakahusay nito, nakapagbibigay ng tatlo o higit pang yunit ng init sa bawat yunit ng kuryente, at mahalaga ito sa pagpapalit sa mga boiler na gumagamit ng gas at langis."});

ADDART({id:"lidar",t:"Lidar",c:"Technology",i:"📡",w:"Lidar",r:["laser","radar","selfdriving","maya","smartphone"],
p:[["Measuring with lasers","Lidar measures distances by timing reflected laser pulses."],["Seeing through forests","Airborne lidar reveals ruins hidden under jungle."],["Eyes of robots","Lidar helps self-driving cars and robots see in 3D."]],
l:"Lidar, short for ‘light detection and ranging’, is a technology that measures distances by firing pulses of laser light and timing how long they take to bounce back from objects. Because light travels at a known speed, each returning pulse gives a precise distance, and a scanner firing hundreds of thousands of pulses per second can build a detailed three-dimensional ‘point cloud’ of its surroundings. Lidar was developed in the 1960s, soon after the invention of the laser; in 1971, Apollo 15 astronauts used a laser altimeter to map the Moon’s surface. Lidar mounted on aircraft and drones maps landscapes, forests, floodplains and coastlines, and because some pulses pass through gaps in leaves, it can reveal the ground beneath dense forest. This has led to spectacular archaeological discoveries, including thousands of hidden Maya structures in Central America. Lidar is used in self-driving cars, robots, smartphones and tablets for depth sensing, and by scientists to study clouds, air pollution and ice sheets.",
s:[["How it works","A lidar sensor sends a laser pulse and measures its return time, often within a billionth of a second. Combined with GPS and motion sensors that record the device’s exact position and orientation, the measurements form a precise 3D map. Some pulses return from the treetops, others from branches, and some from the ground."],["Lost cities","In 2018, archaeologists announced that airborne lidar over northern Guatemala had revealed more than 60,000 previously unknown Maya structures, including houses, causeways and fortifications. Later surveys in Mexico, the Amazon and Cambodia, around Angkor, uncovered whole hidden cities."],["Self-driving cars","Many autonomous vehicles use spinning or solid-state lidar units on their roofs to detect cars, pedestrians and obstacles in 3D, day or night. Some companies, such as Tesla, rely on cameras instead, arguing lidar is unnecessary; the debate continues."],["Science from space","NASA’s ICESat-2 satellite, launched in 2018, uses lidar to measure the height of ice sheets and sea ice to within a few centimeters, tracking how polar ice is melting. Space lidar also measures forest heights to estimate how much carbon they store."]],
k:[["Stands for","Light detection and ranging"],["Uses","Laser pulses"],["Output","3D point clouds"],["Famous discovery","60,000+ hidden Maya structures (2018)"],["Space example","ICESat-2 (2018)"]],
d:[["1960","First laser built"],["1971","Apollo 15 maps the Moon with a laser altimeter"],["2005","Lidar-equipped robot cars compete in the DARPA Grand Challenge"],["2018","Lidar reveals 60,000 Maya structures in Guatemala"],["2018","ICESat-2 launched"],["2020","Lidar sensors added to smartphones and tablets"]],
s2:[["Lidar in your pocket","Some smartphones and tablets released from 2020 include small lidar scanners that help cameras focus in the dark, measure rooms and place augmented reality objects accurately."],["Radar, sonar and lidar","Radar uses radio waves, sonar uses sound and lidar uses light. Lidar gives much finer detail than radar but is affected more by fog, rain and dust."]],
sp:["Angkor’s hidden cities","Lidar surveys in Cambodia in 2012 and 2015 revealed that the area around Angkor Wat once contained a vast urban landscape of roads, canals and neighborhoods hidden under forest, much larger than previously known."],
g:[["Laser","A device producing a narrow, intense beam of light"],["Point cloud","A set of 3D points representing a surface"],["Altimeter","A device that measures height"],["Autonomous vehicle","A self-driving vehicle"]],
f:["Lidar measures distances with laser pulses.","Lidar revealed tens of thousands of hidden Maya structures.","Some smartphones contain lidar scanners."],
fun:"Apollo astronauts left mirrors on the Moon, and scientists still bounce lasers off them to measure the Moon’s distance to within millimeters.",
tl:"Sinusukat ng lidar ang layo sa pamamagitan ng pagbilang ng oras ng bumabalik na mga pulso ng laser. Ibinubunyag ng lidar mula sa eroplano ang mga guho sa ilalim ng gubat, at tumutulong ito sa mga self-driving car at robot na makakita nang 3D."});

ADDART({id:"pacemakers",t:"Pacemakers",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💓",w:"Artificial cardiac pacemaker",r:["heart","heartdisease","surgery","battery","prosthetics"],
p:[["Keeping the beat","Pacemakers send electrical pulses to keep the heart beating regularly."],["First implant in 1958","The first implantable pacemaker was fitted in Sweden in 1958."],["Smaller every year","Modern leadless pacemakers are smaller than a coin."]],
l:"An artificial pacemaker is a small medical device that uses electrical pulses to make the heart beat at a normal rate when its natural pacemaker, a cluster of cells called the sinoatrial node, or its electrical pathways do not work properly. Conditions such as an abnormally slow heartbeat or heart block can cause tiredness, dizziness and fainting. A typical pacemaker has a battery-powered generator placed under the skin below the collarbone and one or more thin wires, called leads, threaded through veins into the heart. It monitors the heart’s rhythm and sends a pulse only when needed. The first external pacemakers of the 1950s were bulky machines plugged into the wall. In 1958, Swedish engineer Rune Elmqvist and surgeon Åke Senning implanted the first fully internal pacemaker in Arne Larsson, who went on to receive 26 devices and lived until 2001. Today more than a million pacemakers are implanted each year worldwide. Batteries last about 7 to 15 years, and leadless pacemakers implanted directly into the heart were approved in 2016.",
s:[["How it works","Sensors in the pacemaker detect the heart’s electrical activity. If the heart beats too slowly or pauses, the pacemaker sends a tiny pulse that makes the heart muscle contract. Many modern pacemakers can increase the heart rate during exercise by sensing movement or breathing."],["Arne Larsson","Arne Larsson, a 43-year-old Swedish engineer, had a heart condition that caused him to faint many times a day. His wife persuaded doctors to try an experimental implanted pacemaker. The first device lasted only hours, but he lived another 43 years with later pacemakers, outliving both his surgeon and the engineer."],["Leadless pacemakers","Leadless pacemakers, about the size of a large vitamin capsule, are inserted through a vein in the leg and attached directly inside the heart. They avoid problems with leads, such as breakage and infection. Wireless monitoring lets doctors check pacemakers remotely."],["Defibrillators","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICDs) are related devices that detect dangerous fast heart rhythms and deliver an electric shock to restore a normal beat. Many devices combine pacemaker and defibrillator functions."]],
k:[["Purpose","Keeps the heart beating at a normal rate"],["First implant","Sweden, 1958"],["First patient","Arne Larsson"],["Battery life","About 7–15 years"],["Leadless pacemakers","Approved 2016"]],
d:[["1932","Albert Hyman builds an early external pacemaker"],["1950","External pacemaker developed in Canada"],["1958","First implantable pacemaker"],["1960","First long-lasting implantable pacemaker in the US"],["2016","First leadless pacemaker approved in the US"]],
s2:[["Pacemakers and phones","Strong magnets and some electronic devices can interfere with pacemakers. Patients are advised to keep phones and magnetic earbuds at least about 15 centimeters from the device. Modern pacemakers are well shielded from most everyday electronics."],["Nuclear pacemakers","In the 1970s, some pacemakers were powered by small amounts of plutonium-238, giving batteries that lasted decades. A few patients still had working nuclear pacemakers in the 2000s, but lithium batteries replaced them."]],
sp:["Wilson Greatbatch","American engineer Wilson Greatbatch invented a practical implantable pacemaker in 1958 after accidentally fitting the wrong resistor into a heart-recording device, which produced regular pulses. He later developed long-lasting lithium batteries for pacemakers."],
g:[["Sinoatrial node","The heart’s natural pacemaker"],["Lead","A wire carrying pulses to the heart"],["Heart block","A blockage of the heart’s electrical signals"],["Defibrillator","A device that shocks the heart into a normal rhythm"]],
f:["The first implanted pacemaker was fitted in 1958.","Arne Larsson received 26 pacemakers.","Leadless pacemakers are smaller than a coin."],
fun:"Arne Larsson, the first person to receive an implanted pacemaker, lived to the age of 86.",
tl:"Nagpapadala ang pacemaker ng mga pulsong elektrikal upang mapanatiling regular ang tibok ng puso. Inilagay ang unang implantable pacemaker sa Sweden noong 1958, at mas maliit pa sa barya ang mga makabagong leadless pacemaker."});

ADDART({id:"fermiparadox",t:"The Fermi Paradox",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👽",w:"Fermi paradox",r:["astrobio","exoplanet","milky","ufos","radioastronomy"],
p:[["Where is everybody?","The Fermi paradox asks why we see no sign of alien civilizations."],["A big galaxy","The Milky Way has billions of stars and planets, many possibly habitable."],["Many possible answers","Explanations range from rare life to aliens choosing to hide."]],
l:"The Fermi paradox is the apparent contradiction between the high probability that intelligent alien civilizations exist and the lack of any evidence for them. It is named after Italian-American physicist Enrico Fermi, who, during a lunch conversation at Los Alamos in 1950, reportedly asked, ‘But where is everybody?’ The argument runs that the Milky Way contains hundreds of billions of stars, many with planets in habitable zones, and is about 13 billion years old; even a civilization traveling far slower than light could have spread across the galaxy in a few million years. Yet we have found no alien signals, probes or visitors. In 1961, astronomer Frank Drake devised the Drake equation to estimate the number of communicating civilizations, though most of its factors are unknown. Proposed solutions include that life or intelligence is extremely rare (the ‘Rare Earth’ hypothesis), that civilizations destroy themselves or face a ‘Great Filter’, that interstellar travel is impractical, that aliens are deliberately avoiding us, or that we have not searched long or carefully enough. The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence (SETI) continues to look for signals.",
s:[["The Drake equation","The Drake equation multiplies the rate of star formation, the fraction of stars with planets, the number of habitable planets per system, and the fractions where life, intelligence and communication arise, by how long civilizations last. Astronomers now know many of the astronomical factors, but the biological ones remain guesses ranging from almost zero to very high."],["The Great Filter","Economist Robin Hanson proposed in 1996 that some step from dead planet to galaxy-spanning civilization must be extremely hard: a ‘Great Filter’. If it lies in our past, such as the origin of life, we may be rare; if it lies ahead, it might threaten our future."],["Other explanations","Some suggest that civilizations communicate in ways we do not recognize, that they are too far apart in space and time, or that the galaxy is quiet because advanced life avoids broadcasting. The ‘zoo hypothesis’ imagines aliens watching but not interfering. Some argue that intelligence like ours may simply be unusual."],["Searching","SETI projects have listened for radio signals since Frank Drake’s Project Ozma in 1960. The 1977 ‘Wow! signal’ remains unexplained but was never detected again. The Breakthrough Listen project, launched in 2015, is the largest search so far. Astronomers also look for ‘technosignatures’ such as unusual light or heat from distant stars."]],
k:[["Named after","Enrico Fermi (1950)"],["Key question","‘Where is everybody?’"],["Drake equation","1961"],["Famous unexplained signal","Wow! signal (1977)"],["Largest search","Breakthrough Listen (since 2015)"]],
d:[["1950","Fermi asks ‘Where is everybody?’"],["1960","Project Ozma, the first SETI radio search"],["1961","Frank Drake presents his equation"],["1977","The Wow! signal is detected"],["1996","Robin Hanson proposes the Great Filter"],["2015","Breakthrough Listen begins"]],
s2:[["Rare Earth","In their 2000 book Rare Earth, Peter Ward and Donald Brownlee argued that simple life may be common but complex life is rare, because it needs a long list of fortunate conditions, such as a stable star, a large moon, plate tectonics and protection from comet impacts by Jupiter."],["Should we call out?","Some scientists argue that sending powerful messages to other stars, called METI, could be risky if alien civilizations were hostile, while others say any advanced civilization could already detect us. The debate has no clear answer."]],
sp:["The Wow! signal","On 15 August 1977, the Big Ear radio telescope in Ohio detected a strong, narrow radio signal from the constellation Sagittarius. Astronomer Jerry Ehman circled it on the printout and wrote ‘Wow!’. It lasted 72 seconds and was never heard again."],
g:[["Paradox","A situation that seems contradictory"],["SETI","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence"],["Habitable zone","The region around a star where liquid water could exist"],["Technosignature","Evidence of technology from another civilization"]],
f:["Fermi asked ‘Where is everybody?’ in 1950.","The Drake equation estimates alien civilizations.","The Wow! signal was never detected again."],
fun:"Even traveling at a small fraction of the speed of light, a civilization could in theory visit every star system in the Milky Way within a few million years, a short time compared with the galaxy’s age.",
tl:"Tinatanong ng Fermi paradox kung bakit wala tayong nakikitang tanda ng sibilisasyon ng mga alien. Bilyun-bilyon ang bituin at planeta sa Milky Way, at maraming posibleng paliwanag, mula sa pagiging bihira ng buhay hanggang sa sadyang pagtatago ng mga alien."});

ADDART({id:"terraforming",t:"Terraforming",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"Terraforming",r:["mars","venus","climchange","scifi","marsrovers"],
p:[["Making new Earths","Terraforming means changing a planet to make it habitable for humans."],["Mars is the main target","Most ideas focus on warming and thickening Mars’s atmosphere."],["Science fiction for now","Terraforming would take centuries or longer, if it is possible at all."]],
l:"Terraforming, meaning ‘Earth-shaping’, is the hypothetical process of deliberately changing the atmosphere, temperature, surface and ecology of a planet or moon to make it habitable for humans and other Earth life. The word was coined by science fiction writer Jack Williamson in 1942, and the idea was explored scientifically by astronomer Carl Sagan, who suggested in 1961 that algae could alter the atmosphere of Venus. Mars is the most discussed target: it has water ice, a day almost as long as Earth’s and a thin carbon dioxide atmosphere. Proposals to warm Mars include releasing greenhouse gases, placing mirrors in orbit to reflect sunlight onto the poles or spreading engineered particles to trap heat, which could eventually allow liquid water and plants. However, a 2018 NASA-funded study found that Mars does not have enough accessible carbon dioxide to thicken its atmosphere to Earth-like pressure, and Mars lacks a global magnetic field to protect an atmosphere. Venus would need its crushing, scorching atmosphere removed. Many scientists consider terraforming far beyond current technology, and some raise ethical questions about altering other worlds.",
s:[["Warming Mars","Mars’s average temperature is about −60°C, and its air pressure is less than 1 percent of Earth’s. A 2024 study suggested that releasing billions of tiny engineered nanorods of iron or aluminum into the Martian atmosphere could warm the planet by more than 30°C, enough for liquid water in places, much more efficiently than greenhouse gases. It would still take decades or more."],["Obstacles","Even if Mars were warmed, its atmosphere would remain too thin to breathe without oxygen, and building up oxygen through plants or microbes could take thousands of years. Without a magnetic field, the solar wind slowly strips the atmosphere away. Low gravity, about 38 percent of Earth’s, may also affect human health."],["Venus and others","Venus is similar in size to Earth, but its carbon dioxide atmosphere is 90 times thicker and its surface hot enough to melt lead. Ideas such as giant sunshades are extremely speculative. Some suggest that floating cities high in Venus’s atmosphere, where temperature and pressure are Earth-like, might be easier than terraforming."],["Ethics","If Mars has native microbes, terraforming could destroy them. Planetary protection rules aim to avoid contaminating other worlds. Some argue that humans should first take care of Earth, while others see terraforming as a long-term way to ensure humanity’s survival."]],
k:[["Meaning","‘Earth-shaping’"],["Word coined","Jack Williamson, 1942"],["Main target","Mars"],["Mars air pressure","Less than 1% of Earth’s"],["2018 study","Not enough CO₂ to thicken Mars’s air"]],
d:[["1942","Jack Williamson coins ‘terraforming’"],["1961","Carl Sagan proposes changing Venus with algae"],["1976","NASA studies the habitability of Mars"],["1992","Kim Stanley Robinson’s Red Mars published"],["2018","Study finds Mars lacks enough CO₂ to terraform easily"],["2024","Study proposes warming Mars with nanorods"]],
s2:[["Terraforming in fiction","Kim Stanley Robinson’s Mars trilogy (1992–1996) describes centuries of terraforming Mars and the political conflicts it causes. Terraforming also appears in films and games such as Total Recall and Terraforming Mars."],["Practice on Earth?","Some scientists see geoengineering proposals on Earth, such as blocking some sunlight to cool the planet, as a kind of small-scale terraforming, and warn that the side effects of changing a planet are hard to predict."]],
sp:["Paraterraforming","Rather than changing a whole planet, ‘paraterraforming’ would enclose smaller areas, such as craters or canyons, under domes or roofs with breathable air, creating habitable spaces more quickly."],
g:[["Terraforming","Changing a planet to be like Earth"],["Greenhouse gas","A gas that traps heat"],["Planetary protection","Rules to avoid contaminating other worlds"],["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles from the Sun"]],
f:["The word terraforming was coined in 1942.","Mars’s air pressure is less than 1% of Earth’s.","Terraforming would take centuries or more."],
fun:"Some terraforming ideas propose crashing icy comets into Mars to add water and gases to its atmosphere.",
tl:"Ang terraforming ay pagbabago sa isang planeta upang matirhan ito ng tao. Karamihan ng ideya ay nakatuon sa pagpapainit at pagpapakapal ng atmospera ng Mars, ngunit aabutin ito ng daan-daang taon o higit pa, kung posible man."});

ADDART({id:"spaceelevators",t:"Space Elevators",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛗",w:"Space elevator",r:["elevators","rocket","sat","space","scifi"],
p:[["A cable to space","A space elevator would be a cable reaching from Earth into orbit."],["Cheaper than rockets","Climbers could carry cargo into space using electricity."],["No material yet","No known material is strong enough to build one on Earth."]],
l:"A space elevator is a proposed structure that would connect Earth’s surface to space with an extremely long cable, or tether, along which vehicles called climbers could travel into orbit without rockets. The cable would be anchored near the equator and extend beyond geostationary orbit, about 36,000 kilometers up, to a counterweight farther out, perhaps 100,000 kilometers away; Earth’s rotation would keep the cable taut, like a stone whirled on a string. Russian scientist Konstantin Tsiolkovsky imagined a tower to space in 1895, and engineer Yuri Artsutanov proposed the tether concept in 1960. Arthur C. Clarke popularized it in his 1979 novel The Fountains of Paradise. A space elevator could greatly reduce the cost of reaching orbit, but the cable would need to be stronger and lighter than any existing material. Carbon nanotubes and graphene might theoretically be strong enough, but they cannot yet be made in long enough, flawless lengths. A space elevator on the Moon or Mars, with lower gravity, would be easier and might be built with existing materials.",
s:[["How it would work","Climbers would grip the cable and climb using electric motors, powered by lasers beamed from the ground or by solar panels. A payload released above geostationary orbit would be flung outward with enough speed to reach other planets. A trip to geostationary orbit might take about a week."],["The material problem","The cable would have to hold its own enormous weight. Steel would snap under its own weight long before reaching orbit. Calculations show it would need a strength-to-weight ratio far beyond steel or Kevlar. Carbon nanotubes are about 100 times stronger than steel by weight in tiny samples, but long cables are another matter."],["Dangers","A space elevator would face lightning, storms, corrosion from atomic oxygen, impacts from space debris and satellites, and possible sabotage. If the cable broke, parts could fall to Earth or be flung into space. Its anchor could be on a movable ocean platform to dodge debris."],["Plans and competitions","NASA held space elevator competitions for tethers and climbers from 2005 to 2009. The Japanese company Obayashi announced a concept to build a space elevator by 2050. The International Space Elevator Consortium studies the idea."]],
k:[["Main parts","Cable, anchor, counterweight, climbers"],["Height","Beyond 36,000 km (geostationary orbit)"],["Early idea","Tsiolkovsky, 1895"],["Tether concept","Yuri Artsutanov, 1960"],["Possible materials","Carbon nanotubes, graphene"]],
d:[["1895","Tsiolkovsky imagines a tower to space"],["1960","Artsutanov proposes a space tether"],["1975","Jerome Pearson publishes a detailed design"],["1979","Arthur C. Clarke’s The Fountains of Paradise"],["2005","NASA begins space elevator competitions"],["2012","Obayashi announces a 2050 concept"]],
s2:[["Inspired by the Eiffel Tower","Tsiolkovsky was inspired by the Eiffel Tower, completed in 1889, to imagine a much taller tower reaching geostationary orbit, with a ‘celestial castle’ at the top."],["Lunar elevator","Because the Moon’s gravity is only one-sixth of Earth’s and it has no atmosphere, a lunar space elevator could be built with strong existing materials such as Zylon or Kevlar, linking the surface to a point in space."]],
sp:["Clarke’s novel","In The Fountains of Paradise, Arthur C. Clarke imagined a space elevator rising from a mountain on an island based on Sri Lanka, where he lived. He said space elevators would be built ‘about 50 years after everyone stops laughing’."],
g:[["Tether","A long cable"],["Geostationary orbit","An orbit that matches Earth’s rotation, about 36,000 km up"],["Counterweight","A mass that balances another"],["Carbon nanotube","A tiny, extremely strong tube of carbon atoms"]],
f:["A space elevator would reach beyond 36,000 km.","No material is yet strong enough to build one.","Arthur C. Clarke popularized the idea in 1979."],
fun:"A space elevator cable could be thinner than a sheet of paper but wider than a ribbon, a design meant to resist damage from small debris.",
tl:"Ang space elevator ay mungkahing kable na aabot mula sa Earth hanggang sa orbit. Maaari itong magdala ng karga sa kalawakan gamit ang kuryente, ngunit wala pang materyal na sapat ang tibay upang itayo ito."});

ADDART({id:"keplertelescope",t:"The Kepler Space Telescope",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Kepler space telescope",r:["exoplanet","keplerlaws","scope","jwst","astrobio"],
p:[["Planet hunter","Kepler searched for planets around other stars."],["Thousands of discoveries","It found more than 2,600 confirmed exoplanets."],["Planets are common","Kepler showed that most stars have planets."]],
l:"The Kepler space telescope was a NASA observatory launched on 6 March 2009 to discover planets orbiting other stars, especially Earth-sized planets in the habitable zone where liquid water could exist. Named after the astronomer Johannes Kepler, it stared continuously at a single patch of sky in the constellations Cygnus and Lyra, monitoring the brightness of about 150,000 stars. When a planet passed in front of its star, called a transit, the star dimmed very slightly, and Kepler’s sensitive camera detected the dip. After two of its four reaction wheels, which pointed the telescope, failed by 2013, engineers devised a new mission called K2, using the pressure of sunlight to help balance the spacecraft. Kepler ran out of fuel and was retired on 30 October 2018. It discovered more than 2,600 confirmed exoplanets, more than any other telescope at the time, and thousands more candidates. Its results showed that planets are extremely common, that small planets are more common than giant ones, and that billions of Earth-sized planets may orbit in habitable zones in the Milky Way.",
s:[["The transit method","Kepler measured stars’ brightness every 30 minutes. A planet the size of Earth passing in front of a Sun-like star dims it by only about 0.01 percent, like a flea crossing a car headlight. Repeated dips at regular intervals revealed a planet and its orbital period, and the size of the dip revealed its size."],["Famous discoveries","Kepler-22b, announced in 2011, was the first planet found in the habitable zone of a Sun-like star. Kepler-186f, in 2014, was the first Earth-sized planet in a habitable zone. Kepler-16b orbits two stars, like Tatooine in Star Wars. Kepler-452b was called Earth’s ‘older cousin’."],["K2","With only two reaction wheels, Kepler could not hold steady. Engineers pointed it along its orbit so that sunlight pressed evenly on it, and it observed different fields for about 80 days each. K2 found hundreds more planets and studied supernovae and comets."],["Legacy","NASA’s TESS, launched in 2018, continues the search across almost the whole sky, finding planets around nearby bright stars. The James Webb Space Telescope studies the atmospheres of some planets found by these missions."]],
k:[["Launched","6 March 2009"],["Retired","30 October 2018"],["Method","Transit"],["Confirmed planets","More than 2,600"],["Stars watched","About 150,000 (main mission)"]],
d:[["2009","Kepler launches"],["2011","Kepler-22b announced in a habitable zone"],["2013","Second reaction wheel fails"],["2014","K2 mission begins; Kepler-186f announced"],["2018","Kepler retired"]],
s2:[["Tabby’s Star","In 2015, citizen scientists studying Kepler data found a star, KIC 8462852, that dimmed irregularly by up to 22 percent. Speculation about alien ‘megastructures’ made headlines, but further studies suggested dust was the cause."],["Citizen science","Volunteers in the Planet Hunters project searched Kepler data by eye and found planets that computer programs had missed, including a planet orbiting in a four-star system."]],
sp:["William Borucki","NASA scientist William Borucki proposed the Kepler mission in the 1980s and had it rejected several times because many doubted that the technology could detect such tiny dips in brightness. He persisted for nearly 20 years before it was approved."],
g:[["Exoplanet","A planet orbiting a star other than the Sun"],["Transit","A planet passing in front of its star"],["Habitable zone","Where liquid water could exist on a planet"],["Reaction wheel","A spinning wheel used to point a spacecraft"]],
f:["Kepler found more than 2,600 confirmed exoplanets.","Kepler showed that most stars have planets.","Kepler watched about 150,000 stars at once."],
fun:"Kepler’s detector could notice a dimming as small as that caused by a flea passing in front of a car headlight seen from far away.",
tl:"Naghanap ang Kepler space telescope ng mga planeta sa paligid ng ibang bituin. Nakatuklas ito ng mahigit 2,600 kumpirmadong exoplanet at ipinakita nitong karamihan ng bituin ay may mga planeta."});

ADDART({id:"vesta",t:"Vesta",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪨",w:"4 Vesta",r:["asteroidbelt","ceres","ionengines","meteors","impactcraters"],
p:[["A giant asteroid","Vesta is the second-largest object in the asteroid belt."],["A planet that never finished","It has a core, mantle and crust like a small planet."],["Pieces on Earth","Some meteorites that fall to Earth come from Vesta."]],
l:"Vesta is one of the largest objects in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, about 525 kilometers across, second in mass only to the dwarf planet Ceres. It was discovered on 29 March 1807 by German astronomer Heinrich Olbers and named after the Roman goddess of the hearth. Vesta is the brightest asteroid as seen from Earth and can occasionally be seen with the naked eye. Unlike most asteroids, which are rubble piles, Vesta is a protoplanet: early in the solar system’s history it melted and separated into an iron core, a rocky mantle and a basaltic crust, like the rocky planets, but it never grew into a full planet. Its southern hemisphere is dominated by two enormous overlapping impact basins, Rheasilvia and Veneneia; Rheasilvia is about 500 kilometers wide and has a central peak about 20 kilometers high, one of the tallest mountains in the solar system. Debris from these impacts is the source of the HED meteorites, about 5 percent of meteorites that fall on Earth. NASA’s Dawn spacecraft orbited Vesta in 2011–2012, mapping it in detail.",
s:[["Dawn at Vesta","Dawn arrived at Vesta in July 2011 and orbited for 14 months, mapping its surface, measuring its gravity and studying its minerals with its cameras and spectrometers, before using its ion engines to fly on to Ceres."],["Rheasilvia","The Rheasilvia impact, about 1 billion years ago, blasted out about 1 percent of Vesta’s volume. Its central peak rises about 20 to 25 kilometers, comparable to Olympus Mons on Mars. The impact also created huge troughs that circle Vesta’s equator."],["Vesta meteorites","HED meteorites (howardites, eucrites and diogenites) have compositions matching Vesta’s crust. They allow scientists to study pieces of a protoplanet in the laboratory, revealing how Vesta melted and formed layers within a few million years of the birth of the solar system."],["Asteroid or protoplanet?","Vesta is not round enough to be classified as a dwarf planet, partly because of its giant impact basins, but its internal layers make it unlike typical asteroids. Scientists often call it the last surviving protoplanet of its kind."]],
k:[["Diameter","About 525 km"],["Discovered","Heinrich Olbers, 29 March 1807"],["Type","Protoplanet"],["Giant basin","Rheasilvia, about 500 km wide"],["Visited by","Dawn (2011–2012)"]],
d:[["1807","Olbers discovers Vesta"],["1970","Link between Vesta and HED meteorites proposed"],["1997","Hubble reveals a huge crater at Vesta’s south pole"],["2011","Dawn enters orbit around Vesta"],["2012","Dawn departs for Ceres"]],
s2:[["A snowman of craters","Three craters lined up on Vesta, nicknamed the ‘Snowman’, became one of the most recognizable features in Dawn’s images."],["Seeing Vesta","When Vesta is at its brightest, it can reach about magnitude 5.2, just bright enough to see with the naked eye from a very dark site. It is the only asteroid regularly visible without a telescope."]],
sp:["Named after a goddess","Vesta was the Roman goddess of the home and hearth, whose sacred fire in Rome was tended by the Vestal Virgins. The asteroid was the fourth to be discovered, after Ceres, Pallas and Juno."],
g:[["Protoplanet","A body that began forming into a planet but did not finish"],["Basin","A very large, shallow crater"],["Meteorite","A space rock that reaches the ground"],["Basalt","A dark volcanic rock"]],
f:["Vesta is the second-largest object in the asteroid belt.","About 5% of meteorites come from Vesta.","Vesta can sometimes be seen with the naked eye."],
fun:"The central peak of Vesta’s Rheasilvia crater is about 20 kilometers high, more than twice as tall as Mount Everest.",
tl:"Ang Vesta ang ikalawang pinakamalaking bagay sa asteroid belt. Mayroon itong core, mantle at crust tulad ng maliit na planeta, at ilang meteorite na bumabagsak sa Earth ay nagmumula rito."});

ADDART({id:"tianwen",t:"China’s Tianwen Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇨🇳",w:"Tianwen-1",r:["mars","marsrovers","spaceagencies","chandrayaan","phobosdeimos"],
p:[["Questions to heaven","Tianwen means ‘questions to heaven’, after an ancient poem."],["Mars in one try","Tianwen-1 orbited, landed and drove on Mars on its first attempt."],["Asteroids and samples","Tianwen-2 reached a near-Earth asteroid in 2026."]],
l:"The Tianwen missions are China’s program of robotic exploration of the planets, run by the China National Space Administration. The name, meaning ‘questions to heaven’, comes from a poem by the ancient Chinese poet Qu Yuan. Tianwen-1, launched on 23 July 2020, was China’s first independent mission to Mars. It entered Mars orbit in February 2021 and, on 15 May 2021, landed the Zhurong rover on the plain of Utopia Planitia, making China the second country, after the United States, to operate a rover on Mars and the first to orbit, land and deploy a rover on its first Mars mission. Zhurong explored for about a year, finding evidence of past water, before going into hibernation in May 2022 and not waking. Tianwen-2, launched on 29 May 2025, reached the small near-Earth asteroid Kamoʻoalewa, a ‘quasi-moon’ of Earth, in mid-2026 and plans to collect samples and return them to Earth before traveling on to a comet. Tianwen-3, planned for launch around 2028, aims to return samples from Mars by about 2031.",
s:[["Tianwen-1","The Tianwen-1 orbiter has mapped Mars and relayed data from the rover. The landing used a heat shield, parachute and rocket-powered descent, the ‘seven minutes of terror’. A small deployable camera photographed the orbiter in space, and Zhurong took a selfie with its lander."],["Zhurong","Named after a Chinese god of fire, the six-wheeled, solar-powered Zhurong rover traveled about 1.9 kilometers. Its ground-penetrating radar found layered deposits beneath the surface, and studies of its data suggested an ancient ocean shoreline in the area. Dust on its solar panels likely prevented it from waking after the Martian winter."],["Tianwen-2","Kamoʻoalewa, discovered in 2016, may be a fragment of the Moon blasted off by an ancient impact. Tianwen-2 is surveying it before attempting to collect samples, which are to be returned to Earth by capsule. The spacecraft will then use Earth’s gravity to head for the main-belt comet 311P/PANSTARRS, arriving in the 2030s."],["Tianwen-3 and beyond","Tianwen-3 plans two launches to land on Mars, collect samples, launch them into Mars orbit and return them to Earth, a mission NASA had also planned but scaled back because of costs. A future Tianwen-4 mission is planned to explore Jupiter’s system."]],
k:[["Name meaning","‘Questions to heaven’"],["Tianwen-1 launch","23 July 2020"],["Zhurong landing","15 May 2021"],["Tianwen-2 target","Asteroid Kamoʻoalewa"],["Mars sample return","Tianwen-3, about 2031"]],
d:[["2020","Tianwen-1 launches"],["2021","Zhurong lands on Mars"],["2022","Zhurong enters hibernation"],["2025","Tianwen-2 launches"],["2026","Tianwen-2 reaches Kamoʻoalewa"],["c. 2028","Tianwen-3 planned launch"]],
s2:[["China’s space program","China has become a major space power, building the Tiangong space station, landing the Chang’e probes on the Moon, including the first landing on the far side in 2019, and returning lunar samples. It plans crewed lunar landings before 2030."],["A quasi-moon","Kamoʻoalewa circles the Sun in an orbit so similar to Earth’s that it appears to loop around our planet as seen from Earth, though it is not truly a moon. It is only about 40 to 100 meters across and spins once every 28 minutes."]],
sp:["Qu Yuan’s questions","The ancient poem Tianwen, attributed to Qu Yuan, who lived about 2,300 years ago, consists of more than 170 questions about the heavens, nature, myth and history, reflecting curiosity about the universe."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that drives on another world"],["Quasi-moon","An asteroid that appears to orbit Earth"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"],["Hibernation","A dormant, inactive state"]],
f:["Tianwen means ‘questions to heaven’.","Zhurong landed on Mars in 2021.","Tianwen-2 reached a quasi-moon of Earth in 2026."],
fun:"Zhurong’s selfie with its lander was taken by a small remote camera the rover dropped on the ground before driving away.",
tl:"Ang Tianwen ay programa ng China sa paggalugad sa mga planeta, at nangangahulugang ‘mga tanong sa langit’. Sa unang subok, nag-orbit, lumapag at nagmaneho sa Mars ang Tianwen-1, at narating ng Tianwen-2 ang isang asteroid noong 2026."});

;
/* data/modern37.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 7): technology.
ADDART({id:"hearingaids",t:"Hearing Aids and Cochlear Implants",c:"Technology",i:"🦻",w:"Hearing aid",r:["ear","signlang","headphones","microphones","prosthetics"],
p:[["Helping people hear","Hearing aids make sounds louder and clearer."],["Bionic ears","Cochlear implants stimulate the hearing nerve directly."],["Now over the counter","Some hearing aids can now be bought without a prescription."]],
l:"Hearing aids are small electronic devices worn in or behind the ear that make sounds louder and clearer for people with hearing loss. A microphone picks up sound, a processor adjusts it for the wearer’s particular pattern of hearing loss, and a tiny speaker delivers it into the ear canal. Before electronics, people used ear trumpets, funnel-shaped horns that gathered sound. Electric hearing aids appeared in the early 20th century, transistor aids small enough to wear on the body arrived in the 1950s, and digital hearing aids, introduced in the 1990s, can reduce background noise and connect wirelessly to phones. For people with severe or profound deafness, cochlear implants can help: they bypass damaged parts of the inner ear and send electrical signals directly to the auditory nerve through electrodes placed in the cochlea. Australian surgeon Graeme Clark implanted the first multichannel cochlear implant in 1978, and more than a million people now use them. The World Health Organization estimates that more than 1.5 billion people live with some hearing loss, but most who need hearing aids do not have them.",
s:[["How hearing aids work","Modern digital hearing aids split sound into frequency bands and amplify each according to the wearer’s hearing test. Directional microphones focus on voices in front, and programs adapt to quiet rooms, restaurants or music. Styles include behind-the-ear, in-the-ear and nearly invisible in-canal aids."],["Cochlear implants","A cochlear implant has an external microphone and processor worn behind the ear, which sends signals through the skin to a receiver implanted under it. The receiver stimulates up to about 22 electrodes in the cochlea. Implants do not restore normal hearing, but with training many users understand speech and use phones. Children born deaf who receive implants early often develop spoken language."],["Over-the-counter aids","In October 2022, the United States allowed hearing aids for mild to moderate hearing loss to be sold over the counter without a prescription, aiming to lower prices. In 2024, Apple added a hearing-aid feature to its AirPods Pro earbuds, approved by US regulators."],["Hearing loss and health","Untreated hearing loss is linked to social isolation, depression and a higher risk of dementia. A 2023 study suggested that hearing aids may slow cognitive decline in older adults at higher risk. Deaf communities emphasize that deafness is also a cultural identity, with sign languages, and that implants are a personal choice."]],
k:[["Hearing aid parts","Microphone, processor, speaker"],["Digital aids","From the 1990s"],["First multichannel cochlear implant","Graeme Clark, 1978"],["Cochlear implant users","More than a million"],["People with hearing loss","More than 1.5 billion (WHO)"]],
d:[["1600s","Ear trumpets used"],["1898","First electric hearing aid"],["1952","Transistor hearing aids introduced"],["1978","First multichannel cochlear implant"],["1996","Fully digital hearing aids launched"],["2022","US allows over-the-counter hearing aids"]],
s2:[["Beethoven’s ear trumpets","Ludwig van Beethoven, who became deaf, used ear trumpets made by Johann Nepomuk Maelzel, the inventor of the metronome. He later communicated mostly through conversation notebooks."],["Hidden hearing aids","In the 19th century, hearing aids were often hidden in fans, hats, canes and even thrones; King John VI of Portugal had a throne with lion-head armrests that funneled sound to him."]],
sp:["Graeme Clark","Graeme Clark was inspired by his father, who was deaf. He developed the multichannel cochlear implant at the University of Melbourne; it was first implanted in Rod Saunders in 1978, and the company Cochlear was founded to produce it."],
g:[["Cochlea","The spiral-shaped hearing organ in the inner ear"],["Auditory nerve","The nerve carrying sound signals to the brain"],["Amplify","To make louder"],["Profound deafness","Very severe hearing loss"]],
f:["More than 1.5 billion people have some hearing loss.","Cochlear implants stimulate the hearing nerve directly.","The US allowed over-the-counter hearing aids in 2022."],
fun:"Some modern hearing aids can stream phone calls and music directly into the ears and translate languages in real time.",
tl:"Pinalalakas at nililinaw ng hearing aid ang mga tunog, habang direktang pinasisigla ng cochlear implant ang ugat ng pandinig. Mabibili na ngayon ang ilang hearing aid nang walang reseta."});

ADDART({id:"ereaders",t:"E-readers",c:"Technology",i:"📖",w:"E-reader",r:["novel","encyclopedias","libraries","print","smartphone"],
p:[["Digital books","E-readers are devices designed for reading electronic books."],["Electronic ink","Their screens look like paper and use little power."],["Changing publishing","E-books changed how people buy and borrow books."]],
l:"An e-reader is a portable electronic device designed mainly for reading digital books, called e-books. Most dedicated e-readers use electronic paper, or e-ink, screens, which reflect light like printed paper instead of glowing like phone screens; they are easy to read in sunlight, cause less eye strain for many readers and use power only when the page changes, so batteries can last weeks. E-ink technology was developed at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in the 1990s. Early e-readers such as the Rocket eBook (1998) and Sony Reader (2006) had limited success, but Amazon’s Kindle, launched in November 2007 with wireless access to an online bookstore, made e-books popular. E-readers can store thousands of books, let readers change text size, look up words and highlight passages. E-book sales grew rapidly in the early 2010s and then leveled off, with printed books remaining popular. Libraries lend e-books digitally. Debates continue over e-book prices, ownership rights, as buyers usually license rather than own e-books, and whether reading on screens affects understanding.",
s:[["Electronic ink","An e-ink screen contains millions of tiny capsules filled with charged white and black particles in fluid. Electric fields move the particles to the top or bottom, forming text. The image stays without power, which is why some e-readers show a picture even when switched off. Color e-ink screens have become available."],["The Kindle","Amazon’s first Kindle sold out in hours in 2007. Its built-in cellular connection let users download books in under a minute without a computer. By 2010, Amazon was selling more e-books than hardcover books in the United States. Other popular e-readers include Kobo and Barnes & Noble’s Nook."],["Accessibility","E-readers help people with poor eyesight by enlarging text, and some read aloud using text-to-speech. Special fonts can help readers with dyslexia. E-books also make books available in remote places without bookstores."],["Ownership","In 2009, Amazon remotely deleted copies of George Orwell’s 1984 and Animal Farm from customers’ Kindles because of a rights problem, an ironic event that highlighted the fact that e-book buyers typically receive a license controlled by the seller."]],
k:[["Screen type","Electronic paper (e-ink)"],["E-ink developed","MIT, 1990s"],["Kindle launched","November 2007"],["Battery life","Often weeks"],["Library lending","E-books borrowed digitally"]],
d:[["1971","Project Gutenberg begins digitizing free books"],["1997","E Ink Corporation founded"],["1998","Rocket eBook released"],["2006","Sony Reader uses e-ink"],["2007","Amazon launches the Kindle"],["2010","Amazon sells more e-books than hardcovers in the US"]],
s2:[["Project Gutenberg","In 1971, Michael Hart typed the US Declaration of Independence into a university computer and shared it, starting Project Gutenberg, which now offers more than 70,000 free e-books of works in the public domain."],["Paper or screen?","Some studies suggest that readers understand long texts slightly better on paper than on screens, especially when reading for information under time pressure, though the differences are small and depend on the reader and the task."]],
sp:["Audiobooks","Alongside e-books, audiobooks have grown rapidly with smartphones and streaming services. Many people now ‘read’ books by listening while commuting or exercising."],
g:[["E-book","A book in digital form"],["E-ink","A paper-like electronic display"],["License","Permission to use something under conditions"],["Public domain","Works no longer protected by copyright"]],
f:["E-ink screens use power only when the page changes.","The Kindle launched in 2007.","Project Gutenberg began in 1971."],
fun:"Amazon once deleted copies of George Orwell’s 1984 from readers’ devices, an event many found fittingly Orwellian.",
tl:"Ang e-reader ay aparatong idinisenyo para sa pagbabasa ng mga digital na libro. Mukhang papel ang screen nito at kaunti lamang ang kuryenteng gamit, at binago ng mga e-book ang paraan ng pagbili at paghiram ng libro."});

ADDART({id:"videocalls",t:"Video Calls",c:"Technology",i:"📹",w:"Videotelephony",r:["internet","phone","smartphone","covid19","remotework"],
p:[["Seeing while talking","Video calls let people see and hear each other in real time."],["A long-imagined invention","Picturephones were demonstrated as early as 1964."],["The pandemic boom","Video calls became part of daily life during COVID-19."]],
l:"Video calling, or videotelephony, is the real-time transmission of moving images and sound between people in different places, letting them see and hear each other. The idea of a ‘telephone with pictures’ appeared in science fiction in the late 19th century. AT&T demonstrated its Picturephone at the 1964 New York World’s Fair and launched a commercial service in 1970, but it was expensive and few people used it. Businesses adopted videoconferencing systems in the 1980s and 1990s. Video calling became widespread with faster internet, webcams and smartphones: Skype added video calls in 2006, Apple introduced FaceTime in 2010, and apps such as Zoom, Microsoft Teams, Google Meet and WhatsApp followed. During the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020, video calls became essential for work, school, medicine, religious services and keeping in touch with family; Zoom’s daily meeting participants rose from about 10 million in December 2019 to more than 300 million in April 2020. Video calls remain central to remote work, though some people report ‘Zoom fatigue’.",
s:[["How it works","A camera and microphone capture video and sound, which software compresses into a stream of data using codecs. The data travels over the internet to other participants, where it is decompressed and displayed. Delays of more than a fraction of a second make conversations awkward, so video calls need fast, stable connections."],["Picturephone","AT&T’s Picturephone had a small black-and-white screen and cost about 160 dollars a month in Pittsburgh in 1970, equivalent to over 1,000 dollars today. It failed commercially because few people had one to call. Decades later, cheap webcams and the internet made the idea practical."],["Pandemic boom","In 2020, schools moved to online classes, doctors offered telehealth appointments, and courts and parliaments met online. Weddings and funerals were streamed. ‘Zoom’ became a verb, and video calls changed expectations about working from home."],["Zoom fatigue","Researchers at Stanford University suggested in 2021 that video calls are tiring because of constant close-up eye contact, seeing oneself on screen, reduced movement and the extra effort of reading facial cues. Turning off self-view and taking breaks can help."]],
k:[["Early demonstration","AT&T Picturephone, 1964"],["Skype video","2006"],["FaceTime","2010"],["Pandemic peak (Zoom)","300 million daily participants (April 2020)"],["Known problem","‘Zoom fatigue’"]],
d:[["1927","Bell Labs demonstrates two-way television"],["1964","Picturephone shown at the New York World’s Fair"],["1970","Picturephone service launched"],["2006","Skype adds video calls"],["2010","Apple introduces FaceTime"],["2020","Video calls boom during the COVID-19 pandemic"]],
s2:[["Video calls in fiction","Science fiction long predicted video phones, from Albert Robida’s ‘telephonoscope’ in the 1880s to the video booth call in 2001: A Space Odyssey (1968) and Dick Tracy’s wristwatch two-way TV."],["Sign language","Video calls have been especially valuable for deaf people, allowing conversations in sign language. Video relay services connect deaf callers with interpreters who relay calls to hearing people."]],
sp:["Calls from space","Astronauts on the International Space Station hold video calls with family, schools and mission control, sometimes with delays of a few seconds as signals travel via satellites."],
g:[["Codec","Software that compresses and decompresses data"],["Webcam","A camera connected to a computer"],["Telehealth","Health care delivered remotely"],["Latency","Delay in data transmission"]],
f:["The Picturephone was demonstrated in 1964.","Zoom grew to 300 million daily participants in 2020.","Video calls help deaf people talk in sign language."],
fun:"In 1927, US Commerce Secretary Herbert Hoover appeared in an early two-way television demonstration between Washington and New York.",
tl:"Pinapayagan ng video call ang mga tao na magkita at magkarinigan nang sabay. Ipinakita ang mga picturephone noon pang 1964, at naging bahagi ng araw-araw na buhay ang video call noong COVID-19."});

ADDART({id:"fuelcells",t:"Fuel Cells",c:"Technology",i:"🔋",w:"Fuel cell",r:["hydrogen","battery","ev","apollo","energytransition"],
p:[["Electricity from hydrogen","Fuel cells turn hydrogen and oxygen into electricity, water and heat."],["No burning","They make power through a chemical reaction, not combustion."],["From Apollo to buses","Fuel cells powered spacecraft and now run some vehicles."]],
l:"A fuel cell is a device that converts the chemical energy of a fuel, usually hydrogen, directly into electricity through a reaction with oxygen, producing only water and heat as byproducts. Unlike a battery, it does not run down or need recharging; it keeps producing electricity as long as fuel and oxygen are supplied. Welsh scientist William Grove demonstrated the principle in 1839 with his ‘gas battery’. Fuel cells were first used practically in the 1960s on NASA’s Gemini and Apollo spacecraft, where they provided electricity and drinking water for astronauts. Today they power some buses, trucks, forklifts, trains and cars, such as the Toyota Mirai and Hyundai Nexo, as well as backup power for data centers and hospitals. Fuel cells are efficient and quiet, but their environmental benefits depend on how the hydrogen is made: most hydrogen today comes from natural gas, releasing carbon dioxide, while ‘green’ hydrogen made with renewable electricity is still costly. Battery electric vehicles have outpaced fuel cell cars, partly because hydrogen fueling stations are scarce.",
s:[["How it works","A fuel cell has two electrodes, an anode and a cathode, separated by an electrolyte. Hydrogen at the anode splits into protons and electrons. The protons pass through the electrolyte, while the electrons travel through an external circuit, providing electric current, before combining with oxygen and protons at the cathode to form water. Many cells are stacked together for more power."],["Types","Proton-exchange membrane fuel cells, used in vehicles, work at low temperatures and start quickly. Solid oxide fuel cells operate at high temperatures and are used for stationary power. Alkaline fuel cells powered the Apollo spacecraft."],["Fuel cells in space","Apollo’s fuel cells produced electricity and water for the crew. On Apollo 13 in 1970, an oxygen tank explosion disabled the fuel cells’ oxygen supply, forcing the astronauts to use the lunar module as a lifeboat. Space Shuttle fuel cells also produced drinking water."],["Fuel cell vehicles","Hydrogen cars can refuel in a few minutes and travel 500 kilometers or more, but there are only around a thousand hydrogen stations worldwide, mostly in Japan, South Korea, China, Germany and California. Fuel cells may be more useful for heavy trucks, ships and trains."]],
k:[["Fuel","Usually hydrogen"],["Byproducts","Water and heat"],["Invented","William Grove, 1839"],["First practical use","NASA spacecraft, 1960s"],["Example cars","Toyota Mirai, Hyundai Nexo"]],
d:[["1839","William Grove demonstrates a gas battery"],["1932","Francis Bacon develops an alkaline fuel cell"],["1965","Fuel cells power the Gemini 5 spacecraft"],["1969","Apollo fuel cells supply power and water on the Moon missions"],["2014","Toyota Mirai fuel cell car launched"],["2018","Hydrogen fuel cell trains enter service in Germany"]],
s2:[["Hydrogen trains","In 2018, Germany introduced the world’s first passenger trains powered by hydrogen fuel cells, the Coradia iLint, on routes without overhead electric lines. Some operators later chose battery trains instead because of hydrogen costs."],["Francis Thomas Bacon","British engineer Francis Thomas Bacon, a descendant of the philosopher Francis Bacon, spent decades developing practical alkaline fuel cells. His designs were licensed for the Apollo program, and President Nixon reportedly told him, ‘Without you, Tom, we wouldn’t have gotten to the Moon.’"]],
sp:["Water for astronauts","The water produced by Apollo’s fuel cells was used for drinking and preparing food, but it sometimes contained hydrogen bubbles, which gave astronauts gas."],
g:[["Electrolyte","A substance that lets ions move between electrodes"],["Anode and cathode","The two electrodes of a cell"],["Green hydrogen","Hydrogen made using renewable electricity"],["Combustion","Burning"]],
f:["Fuel cells produce only water and heat.","Apollo astronauts drank water made by fuel cells.","The first hydrogen passenger trains ran in Germany in 2018."],
fun:"The principle of the fuel cell was discovered in 1839, decades before the internal combustion engine became practical.",
tl:"Ginagawang kuryente, tubig at init ng fuel cell ang hydrogen at oxygen. Hindi ito nagsusunog, at nagpatakbo ito ng mga spacecraft at ngayon ng ilang sasakyan."});

ADDART({id:"exoskeletons",t:"Exoskeletons",c:"Technology",i:"🦿",w:"Powered exoskeleton",r:["robot","prosthetics","nervous","olymp","ai"],
p:[["Wearable robots","Exoskeletons are wearable frames that support or strengthen the body."],["Walking again","Some help people with paralysis stand and walk."],["Lighter work","Workers use exoskeletons to reduce strain when lifting."]],
l:"An exoskeleton, in technology, is a wearable device that fits around the body and supports, protects or strengthens its movements. The name comes from the hard outer skeletons of insects and crabs. Powered exoskeletons use motors, sensors and batteries to move joints such as the hips and knees, while passive exoskeletons use springs and counterweights. Medical exoskeletons help people with spinal cord injuries, strokes or muscle diseases stand, walk and exercise; the US Food and Drug Administration first approved one, the ReWalk, for personal use in 2014. In factories and warehouses, exoskeletons support workers’ backs and shoulders during lifting or overhead work, reducing fatigue and injuries. Armies have tested exoskeletons to help soldiers carry heavy loads. Consumer exoskeletons for hiking were launched in the 2020s. Exoskeletons remain expensive and heavy, and researchers are developing soft ‘exosuits’ made of fabric and cables. Science fiction, from Iron Man to the power loader in the film Aliens, has long imagined them.",
s:[["Medical exoskeletons","A medical exoskeleton straps to the legs and torso. Sensors detect when the user shifts weight or leans, and motors move the hips and knees in a walking pattern. Users often need crutches for balance. Standing and walking can improve circulation, bone health and wellbeing."],["Brain-controlled walking","At the opening of the 2014 FIFA World Cup in Brazil, a paralyzed young man named Juliano Pinto used a brain-controlled exoskeleton developed by Miguel Nicolelis’s team to kick a ball. In 2019, a French man with tetraplegia controlled an exoskeleton using brain implants."],["At work","Passive exoskeletons that support arms during overhead work are used by car manufacturers. Back-support exoskeletons help warehouse workers and nurses lift. Studies show they can reduce muscle strain, though they must fit well and not shift loads to other parts of the body."],["The history","In 1890, Russian inventor Nicholas Yagn patented a device with springs to help soldiers walk and jump. General Electric’s Hardiman, built in the 1960s, was a huge powered suit meant to lift 680 kilograms, but it proved too heavy and unstable."]],
k:[["Types","Powered and passive"],["First FDA-approved personal exoskeleton","ReWalk, 2014"],["Medical use","Helping paralyzed people stand and walk"],["Industrial use","Supporting backs and shoulders"],["Early patent","Nicholas Yagn, 1890"]],
d:[["1890","Nicholas Yagn patents a walking aid"],["1965","General Electric begins the Hardiman project"],["2011","ReWalk used in rehabilitation clinics"],["2014","Brain-controlled exoskeleton at the World Cup; ReWalk approved for home use"],["2019","Man with tetraplegia controls an exoskeleton with brain implants"]],
s2:[["Soft exosuits","Researchers at Harvard developed soft exosuits made of fabric, straps and cables driven by small motors, which assist walking with less weight and bulk. They may help stroke patients and reduce the energy needed for walking and running."],["Exoskeletons in fiction","Iron Man’s armor, the power loader in Aliens (1986) and the mech suits of Edge of Tomorrow show exoskeletons granting superhuman strength. Real devices are much more modest."]],
sp:["Ekso Bionics","Amanda Boxtel, paralyzed in a skiing accident in 1992, became one of the first people to walk using an Ekso exoskeleton in 2010 and became an advocate for the technology."],
g:[["Exoskeleton","A hard outer frame"],["Tetraplegia","Paralysis of all four limbs"],["Passive device","One without motors"],["Rehabilitation","Recovery of abilities after injury"]],
f:["Exoskeletons can help paralyzed people walk.","The first personal exoskeleton was approved in 2014.","GE built a huge powered suit in the 1960s."],
fun:"General Electric’s 1960s Hardiman exoskeleton weighed about 680 kilograms, as much as the load it was meant to lift.",
tl:"Ang exoskeleton ay naisusuot na balangkas na sumusuporta o nagpapalakas sa katawan. Tumutulong ang ilan sa mga taong paralisado na tumayo at maglakad, at ginagamit ito ng mga manggagawa upang gumaan ang pagbubuhat."});

ADDART({id:"maglev",t:"Maglev Trains",c:"Technology",i:"🚄",w:"Maglev",r:["highspeedrail","rail","magnet","chinacountry","japancountry"],
p:[["Floating trains","Maglev trains float above their tracks using magnets."],["Record speeds","A Japanese maglev reached 603 km/h in 2015."],["Few lines","Only a handful of maglev lines operate, mostly in Asia."]],
l:"A maglev, short for magnetic levitation, train floats above a guideway using powerful magnets instead of running on wheels and rails. Magnets also propel and guide the train, using a linear electric motor built into the track. With no wheel friction, maglevs can travel very fast, smoothly and quietly. The idea was patented in the early 20th century, and German engineer Hermann Kemper patented a maglev design in 1934. The first commercial maglev was a slow airport shuttle in Birmingham, England, opened in 1984. The Shanghai Maglev, opened in 2004 using German Transrapid technology, links Pudong Airport to the city at up to 300 kilometers per hour, having run at 430 kilometers per hour for years. Japan’s superconducting SCMaglev set the world speed record for trains of 603 kilometers per hour in 2015. It is planned for the Chuo Shinkansen between Tokyo and Nagoya, but construction delays, including a long dispute over a tunnel in Shizuoka resolved in 2026, have pushed the opening to 2034 or later. Maglevs are costly to build and incompatible with existing railways, limiting their use.",
s:[["How maglev works","Germany’s Transrapid used electromagnets on the train that wrap under the guideway and pull upward, keeping it about 1 centimeter above the track. Japan’s SCMaglev uses superconducting magnets cooled to very low temperatures, which repel coils in the guideway walls and lift the train about 10 centimeters at speed; it runs on rubber wheels at low speeds."],["Shanghai Maglev","The Shanghai Maglev covers 30 kilometers in about seven to eight minutes. It was the world’s first high-speed commercial maglev. A planned extension to Hangzhou was cancelled after residents protested about cost and possible electromagnetic effects."],["Speed records","On 21 April 2015, a seven-car SCMaglev test train reached 603 kilometers per hour on the Yamanashi test line in Japan, the fastest speed ever for a train. China has tested maglevs designed for 600 kilometers per hour."],["Chuo Shinkansen","The Chuo Shinkansen is planned to link Tokyo and Nagoya in about 40 minutes and later Osaka, mostly through tunnels. Originally targeted for 2027, it was delayed by Shizuoka Prefecture’s concerns about water flow from a tunnel under the Southern Alps. The governor approved construction in July 2026."]],
k:[["Stands for","Magnetic levitation"],["Speed record","603 km/h (Japan, 2015)"],["First commercial maglev","Birmingham airport, 1984"],["Shanghai Maglev opened","2004"],["Chuo Shinkansen opening","2034 or later"]],
d:[["1934","Hermann Kemper patents a maglev design"],["1984","Birmingham airport maglev opens"],["2004","Shanghai Maglev opens"],["2006","Transrapid test crash in Germany kills 23"],["2015","SCMaglev reaches 603 km/h"],["2026","Shizuoka approves Chuo Shinkansen tunnel"]],
s2:[["The Lathen accident","In September 2006, a Transrapid test train crashed into a maintenance vehicle on the test track at Lathen, Germany, killing 23 people. The accident, caused by human error, contributed to Germany abandoning plans for domestic maglev lines."],["Hyperloop","The hyperloop concept, popularized by Elon Musk in 2013, would send pods through low-pressure tubes using magnetic levitation. Several companies tested prototypes, but most shut down, and no passenger hyperloop has been built."]],
sp:["Urban maglevs","Slower maglevs serve cities in Asia, including the Linimo line in Aichi, Japan, opened for Expo 2005, and lines in Changsha and Beijing in China and Incheon in South Korea."],
g:[["Levitation","Floating in the air"],["Linear motor","An electric motor that produces straight-line motion"],["Superconductor","A material with no electrical resistance when very cold"],["Guideway","The track a maglev follows"]],
f:["Maglev trains float above their tracks.","A Japanese maglev reached 603 km/h in 2015.","The Shanghai Maglev opened in 2004."],
fun:"Passengers on the Shanghai Maglev can watch a display showing the train’s speed as it zooms toward 300 kilometers per hour.",
tl:"Lumulutang ang maglev train sa ibabaw ng riles gamit ang malalakas na magnet. Umabot sa 603 km/h ang isang maglev sa Japan noong 2015, ngunit iilan lamang ang linya nito, karamihan sa Asya."});

ADDART({id:"syntheticfibers",t:"Nylon and Synthetic Fibers",c:"Technology",i:"🧵",w:"Synthetic fiber",r:["plastics","clothing","weaving","fossilfuels","microplastics"],
p:[["Man-made threads","Synthetic fibers such as nylon and polyester are made from chemicals."],["Nylon stockings","Nylon stockings caused shopping frenzies in the 1940s."],["Most of our clothes","Synthetics now make up most of the world’s fiber production."]],
l:"Synthetic fibers are textile fibers made by people from chemicals, mostly derived from oil and natural gas, rather than from plants or animals. The first fully synthetic fiber, nylon, was invented by Wallace Carothers and his team at the DuPont company in 1935. Nylon was strong, elastic and silky, and when nylon stockings went on sale in the United States in May 1940, millions of pairs were sold within days. During World War II, nylon was diverted to parachutes, ropes and tents. Other synthetics followed: polyester, invented in Britain in 1941; acrylic; and spandex, or elastane, introduced in 1958 and sold as Lycra. Today synthetic fibers, led by polyester, make up about two-thirds of global fiber production, used in clothing, sportswear, carpets, ropes, seat belts and industrial materials. They are cheap, durable and quick-drying, but they are made from fossil fuels, shed microplastic fibers when washed and are hard to recycle, adding to plastic pollution. Fast fashion has greatly increased their use.",
s:[["How synthetic fibers are made","Chemicals are joined into long chain molecules called polymers. The polymer is melted or dissolved and forced through tiny holes in a spinneret, like a showerhead, forming filaments that are cooled, stretched and wound into yarn."],["Wallace Carothers","Carothers led DuPont’s research into polymers, producing neoprene, a synthetic rubber, in 1930 and nylon in 1935. Despite his success, he struggled with depression and died in 1937 before nylon went on sale."],["Nylon riots","Nylon stockings were so popular that, after wartime shortages, crowds of thousands queued and scuffled for them in American cities in 1945 and 1946, events newspapers called ‘nylon riots’."],["Environmental problems","Each wash of synthetic clothing can release hundreds of thousands of microfibers, which pass through wastewater plants into rivers and seas. Polyester takes centuries to break down. Recycled polyester, mostly from plastic bottles, and fiber-catching washing machine filters are partial solutions."]],
k:[["First fully synthetic fiber","Nylon, 1935"],["Inventor","Wallace Carothers (DuPont)"],["Most produced fiber","Polyester"],["Share of world fiber","About two-thirds"],["Problem","Microplastic shedding"]],
d:[["1935","Nylon invented at DuPont"],["1940","Nylon stockings go on sale in the US"],["1941","Polyester invented in Britain"],["1945–1946","‘Nylon riots’ in American cities"],["1958","Spandex invented"],["1965","Kevlar invented by Stephanie Kwolek"]],
s2:[["Kevlar","In 1965, DuPont chemist Stephanie Kwolek invented Kevlar, a fiber five times stronger than steel by weight, used in bulletproof vests, helmets, tires and space equipment. It has saved thousands of police officers’ lives."],["Where the name came from","DuPont said ‘nylon’ was a made-up word. A popular myth claims it combines New York and London, but the company has denied this."]],
sp:["Velcro and nylon","George de Mestral’s hook-and-loop fastener became practical only when he discovered that nylon, sewn under infrared light, formed tough, tiny hooks."],
g:[["Polymer","A long chain molecule made of repeating units"],["Spinneret","A device that forms fibers from liquid polymer"],["Microfiber","A tiny fiber shed from fabric"],["Fast fashion","Cheap clothing made quickly in large amounts"]],
f:["Nylon was invented in 1935.","Polyester is the most produced fiber.","Synthetic clothes shed microplastics when washed."],
fun:"About 4 million pairs of nylon stockings were sold in a few hours on the first day they went on sale nationally in the United States.",
tl:"Ang synthetic fiber gaya ng nylon at polyester ay gawa sa mga kemikal. Nagdulot ng pagkakagulo sa pamimili ang nylon stockings noong 1940s, at ngayon ay synthetic na ang karamihan ng hiblang ginagawa sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"bluetooth",t:"Bluetooth",c:"Technology",i:"🦷",w:"Bluetooth",r:["wifi","headphones","smartphone","radio","smarthome"],
p:[["Short-range wireless","Bluetooth connects devices over short distances without cables."],["Named after a king","It is named after Harald Bluetooth, a Viking king."],["Billions of devices","Earbuds, keyboards, watches and cars use Bluetooth."]],
l:"Bluetooth is a wireless technology standard for exchanging data over short distances, typically up to about 10 meters, using radio waves in the 2.4 gigahertz band. It connects phones to wireless earbuds, speakers, smartwatches, keyboards, mice, game controllers, cars, fitness trackers and medical devices. Bluetooth was developed by the Swedish company Ericsson starting in 1989 as a wireless replacement for cables, and the Bluetooth Special Interest Group, formed in 1998 by Ericsson, IBM, Intel, Nokia and Toshiba, manages the standard. The first consumer Bluetooth device, a hands-free mobile phone headset, appeared in 1999. Bluetooth uses ‘frequency hopping’, rapidly switching between 79 channels to avoid interference. Bluetooth Low Energy, introduced in 2010, lets small devices such as trackers and sensors run for months or years on tiny batteries. The name comes from Harald ‘Bluetooth’ Gormsson, a 10th-century king who united Danish tribes, just as the technology unites devices. More than 5 billion Bluetooth devices are shipped each year.",
s:[["How it works","Bluetooth devices form small networks called piconets, in which one device connects to several others. Pairing creates a secure link, often by confirming a code. Frequency hopping, up to 1,600 times a second, reduces interference from Wi-Fi and microwave ovens, which use the same band."],["The name and logo","Engineer Jim Kardach suggested the name ‘Bluetooth’ in 1997 as a temporary code name after reading a historical novel about Vikings. The logo combines the runes for H and B, Harald Bluetooth’s initials. Harald may have been nicknamed for a dead tooth that looked blue."],["Bluetooth Low Energy","Bluetooth Low Energy powers item trackers, heart-rate monitors, smart locks and contact-tracing apps used during the COVID-19 pandemic. Bluetooth 5, from 2016, increased range and speed, and newer versions support audio sharing to several listeners at once."],["Security","Bluetooth vulnerabilities have occasionally allowed attackers to intercept data or take control of devices, so keeping devices updated matters. Item trackers have raised concerns about stalking, leading manufacturers to add alerts for unknown trackers traveling with a person."]],
k:[["Range","Typically up to about 10 m"],["Frequency","2.4 GHz band"],["Developed by","Ericsson (from 1989)"],["Low Energy version","2010"],["Named after","Harald Bluetooth, Danish king"]],
d:[["1989","Ericsson begins work on short-range radio"],["1998","Bluetooth Special Interest Group founded"],["1999","First Bluetooth headset released"],["2010","Bluetooth Low Energy introduced"],["2016","Bluetooth 5 released"]],
s2:[["Harald Bluetooth","Harald Gormsson ruled Denmark and parts of Norway in the late 10th century. He is remembered for uniting Danish tribes and introducing Christianity, recorded on the Jelling runestones, a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Hedy Lamarr’s legacy","The frequency-hopping technique used in Bluetooth echoes a 1942 patent by actress Hedy Lamarr and composer George Antheil for torpedo guidance."]],
sp:["Audio sharing","Bluetooth LE Audio and Auracast technology, introduced in the 2020s, let one device broadcast sound to many earbuds or hearing aids at once, for example in airports, theaters and gyms."],
g:[["Pairing","Linking two Bluetooth devices securely"],["Frequency hopping","Rapidly switching radio channels"],["Piconet","A small Bluetooth network"],["Rune","A letter of an ancient Germanic alphabet"]],
f:["Bluetooth is named after a Viking king.","The Bluetooth logo combines two runes.","Bluetooth hops channels up to 1,600 times a second."],
fun:"Bluetooth’s name was meant to be temporary until marketers found something better, but no better name was agreed on.",
tl:"Ang Bluetooth ay nag-uugnay ng mga aparato sa maikling distansya nang walang kable. Ipinangalan ito kay Harald Bluetooth, isang haring Viking, at ginagamit ito ng earbuds, keyboard, relo at kotse."});

ADDART({id:"keyboards",t:"Computer Keyboards and Mice",c:"Technology",i:"⌨️",w:"Computer keyboard",r:["typewriter","comp","touchscreens","videogames","spine"],
p:[["Typing and pointing","Keyboards and mice are the main ways people control computers."],["QWERTY’s long life","The QWERTY layout dates back to typewriters of the 1870s."],["Douglas Engelbart’s mouse","The computer mouse was demonstrated in 1968."]],
l:"Computer keyboards and mice are input devices that let people type text and control computers. Keyboards descend from the typewriter: the QWERTY layout, named after the first six letters on the top row, was designed by Christopher Latham Sholes for typewriters sold from 1874 and remains the standard for English despite alternatives such as the Dvorak layout. Early computers used punched cards and teletype terminals, and keyboards connected to screens became standard in the 1970s. The computer mouse was invented by Douglas Engelbart and Bill English at the Stanford Research Institute in the 1960s; Engelbart showed it publicly in 1968 in a famous presentation later called ‘The Mother of All Demos’. The mouse became widespread with graphical interfaces, especially Apple’s Macintosh in 1984. Optical mice replaced ball mice around 2000, and wireless keyboards and mice are now common. Touchscreens and voice assistants have reduced the role of keyboards and mice on phones, but they remain essential for work, writing and gaming. Mechanical keyboards have a dedicated following.",
s:[["QWERTY","A popular story says QWERTY was designed to slow typists down to prevent jams. Historians think Sholes arranged letters partly to separate common letter pairs that jammed his machine and partly based on telegraph operators’ needs. Once people learned it, switching became hard, an example of ‘path dependence’."],["Other layouts","France and Belgium use AZERTY, Germany QWERTZ. The Dvorak layout, patented in 1936, places the most common letters on the home row, but studies disagree about how much faster it is. Languages such as Chinese and Japanese use input methods that convert typed sounds or strokes into characters."],["The mouse","Engelbart’s first mouse was a wooden box with two metal wheels. The name came from its tail-like cable. Xerox PARC developed it further in the 1970s, and Apple licensed ideas for the Lisa and Macintosh. Optical mice track movement with a tiny camera and LED."],["Health","Long hours of typing and mouse use can contribute to repetitive strain injuries, including carpal tunnel syndrome. Ergonomic keyboards, vertical mice, good posture and breaks can help."]],
k:[["Standard layout","QWERTY"],["QWERTY designer","Christopher Latham Sholes"],["Mouse inventors","Douglas Engelbart and Bill English"],["Mouse demonstrated","9 December 1968"],["Mouse made popular","Apple Macintosh, 1984"]],
d:[["1874","QWERTY typewriter goes on sale"],["1936","Dvorak keyboard patented"],["1964","Engelbart and English build the first mouse"],["1968","‘The Mother of All Demos’"],["1984","Macintosh popularizes the mouse"],["1999","Optical mice reach consumers"]],
s2:[["The Mother of All Demos","On 9 December 1968, in San Francisco, Engelbart demonstrated the mouse, windows, hypertext links, video conferencing and collaborative editing in a 90-minute presentation that predicted much of modern computing."],["Mechanical keyboards","Mechanical keyboards use individual switches under each key, giving a distinct feel and sound. Gamers, programmers and writers have revived interest in them, building custom keyboards with colorful keycaps."]],
sp:["The @ and # keys","The @ symbol was on typewriters for accounting. The # key, called the hash, pound or number sign, gained new life with phone menus and, from 2007, social media hashtags."],
g:[["QWERTY","The standard English keyboard layout"],["Input device","A device used to give information to a computer"],["Graphical interface","A screen with windows, icons and pointers"],["Repetitive strain injury","Injury from repeated movements"]],
f:["QWERTY dates back to 1874.","The first mouse was made of wood.","The mouse was first demonstrated in 1968."],
fun:"Engelbart’s team called the mouse a ‘mouse’ because the cord coming out of the back looked like a tail; nobody remembered who first used the name.",
tl:"Ang keyboard at mouse ang pangunahing paraan ng pagkontrol sa computer. Nagmula ang QWERTY layout sa mga makinilya ng 1870s, at ipinakita ang computer mouse noong 1968."});

ADDART({id:"supercomputers",t:"Supercomputers",c:"Technology",i:"🖥️",w:"Supercomputer",r:["comp","chip","ai","weatherforecast","quantumcomp"],
p:[["The fastest computers","Supercomputers perform quintillions of calculations per second."],["Exascale era","Several now exceed one exaflop."],["Science machines","They simulate weather, climate, nuclear physics and medicines."]],
l:"A supercomputer is a computer with performance far beyond that of ordinary computers, used for problems requiring enormous numbers of calculations. Supercomputer speed is measured in floating-point operations per second (FLOPS); the fastest systems now exceed an exaflop, a billion billion calculations per second. The first machines called supercomputers, designed by Seymour Cray, appeared in the 1960s, starting with the CDC 6600 in 1964; the Cray-1 of 1976 became an icon. Modern supercomputers contain tens of thousands of processors and graphics processing units (GPUs) working in parallel, connected by fast networks and cooled by liquid. They are used to forecast weather and model climate, simulate nuclear weapons without testing, design aircraft, study earthquakes and the universe, develop medicines and materials, and train artificial intelligence. The TOP500 list ranks the world’s fastest systems twice a year. In June 2026, China’s LineShine, at the National Supercomputing Centre in Shenzhen, became number one, the first Chinese system at the top since 2017, and it stayed first in November 2026, ahead of the US El Capitan.",
s:[["Measuring speed","TOP500 rankings use the LINPACK benchmark, which measures how fast a computer solves a large set of linear equations. Critics note that it does not reflect all real tasks, especially AI workloads, so other lists measure energy efficiency and AI performance. Some powerful AI systems built by companies are not submitted to the TOP500."],["Exascale","The US Frontier, at Oak Ridge National Laboratory, became the first official exascale supercomputer in May 2022. El Capitan, at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory, took first place in November 2024. By November 2026, five systems on the list exceeded one exaflop."],["Power and cooling","A top supercomputer may use 20 to 30 megawatts of electricity, as much as a small town. Liquid cooling carries heat away from chips, and engineers compete on the Green500 list for energy efficiency."],["Uses","Weather services run supercomputers to make forecasts several times a day. During the COVID-19 pandemic, supercomputers simulated the virus and screened possible drugs. Scientists model galaxy formation, protein folding and fusion reactors."]],
k:[["Speed unit","FLOPS"],["Exaflop","10¹⁸ calculations per second"],["First supercomputer","CDC 6600, 1964"],["No. 1 (November 2026)","LineShine, China (about 2.2 exaflops)"],["No. 2","El Capitan, USA (about 1.8 exaflops)"]],
d:[["1964","CDC 6600, the first supercomputer"],["1976","Cray-1 released"],["1993","First TOP500 list"],["1996","ASCI Red passes a teraflop"],["2008","Roadrunner passes a petaflop"],["2022","Frontier becomes the first exascale supercomputer"],["2026","China’s LineShine takes first place"]],
s2:[["Seymour Cray","Seymour Cray, often called the father of supercomputing, designed the CDC 6600 and founded Cray Research. His Cray-1 had a distinctive C-shaped design with a padded bench around it, nicknamed the world’s most expensive love seat."],["Supercomputers and chess","IBM’s Deep Blue, a specialized supercomputer, defeated world chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997. Today, chess programs on ordinary computers are far stronger than any human."]],
sp:["Your phone vs. a Cray","A modern smartphone is many thousands of times faster than the Cray-1, which performed about 160 million calculations per second and cost millions of dollars."],
g:[["FLOPS","Floating-point operations per second"],["Exascale","Capable of at least one exaflop"],["GPU","A processor that handles many calculations in parallel"],["Benchmark","A standard test of performance"]],
f:["The fastest supercomputers exceed an exaflop.","China’s LineShine was ranked No. 1 in 2026.","The first supercomputer was the CDC 6600."],
fun:"An exaflop computer can do in one second what would take every person on Earth calculating once a second for almost four years.",
tl:"Ang supercomputer ang pinakamabilis na computer, na gumagawa ng quintillion na kalkulasyon bawat segundo. Ginagamit ito sa pag-aaral ng panahon, klima, physics at gamot, at nanguna ang LineShine ng China noong 2026."});

ADDART({id:"rfid",t:"RFID and NFC",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Radio-frequency identification",r:["qrcodes","digitalpay","radio","dataprivacy","pets"],
p:[["Tags that talk","RFID tags store information that can be read by radio waves."],["Tap to pay","NFC, a kind of RFID, lets phones and cards pay with a tap."],["Everywhere","RFID tracks goods, pets, passports and toll payments."]],
l:"Radio-frequency identification (RFID) is a technology that uses radio waves to identify and track tags attached to objects, animals or people. An RFID tag contains a tiny chip and an antenna; when a reader sends out a radio signal, a passive tag uses the signal’s energy to power itself and reply with its stored information, without a battery or a direct line of sight. Active tags have their own batteries and longer range. RFID grew from World War II ‘identify friend or foe’ systems that recognized aircraft by radio. Today it is used in shop security tags, warehouse inventory, library books, toll-road payment, building access cards, public transport cards, microchips implanted in pets and electronic passports. Near-field communication (NFC), developed in the early 2000s, is a short-range form of RFID that works within a few centimeters and lets smartphones and bank cards make contactless payments, share data and act as keys. RFID has raised privacy concerns because tags can sometimes be read without the owner knowing.",
s:[["How it works","Passive RFID tags can be as small as a grain of rice. Low-frequency tags work over centimeters, high-frequency tags over about a meter and ultra-high-frequency tags over several meters, allowing whole pallets of goods to be scanned at once as they pass through a warehouse door."],["Contactless payments","Contactless bank cards and phone wallets use NFC with encryption so that each payment uses a one-time code. Contactless payments spread rapidly in the 2010s and especially during the COVID-19 pandemic, when people avoided touching payment terminals."],["Pets and animals","Pet microchips, inserted under the skin, store an identification number that vets and shelters can scan to reunite lost animals with owners. Farmers use RFID ear tags to track livestock, and scientists tag fish and birds to study migration."],["Privacy","Critics worried that RFID tags in clothing or passports could let people be tracked. Electronic passports use encryption and shielded covers. A few people have chosen to have RFID chips implanted in their hands to open doors and pay, a practice that remains rare."]],
k:[["Stands for","Radio-frequency identification"],["Passive tags","No battery, powered by the reader"],["NFC range","A few centimeters"],["Uses","Payments, passports, pets, inventory, tolls"],["Origin","World War II aircraft identification"]],
d:[["1940s","‘Identify friend or foe’ radio systems"],["1973","First RFID patents"],["1990s","RFID toll collection spreads"],["2004","NFC Forum founded"],["2006","Electronic passports introduced in the US"],["2014","Apple Pay launches NFC phone payments"]],
s2:[["The Thing","In 1945, Soviet schoolchildren presented the US ambassador in Moscow with a carved Great Seal of the United States. Hidden inside was ‘The Thing’, a passive listening device invented by Léon Theremin that was powered by radio waves beamed from outside, a forerunner of RFID. It was discovered only in 1952."],["Barcodes and RFID","Unlike barcodes, RFID tags can be read without being seen and many at once, and they can store more information. They are more expensive, so barcodes remain common on everyday products."]],
sp:["Smart shelves","Some clothing stores use RFID tags on every item so that staff can count stock in minutes and customers can use self-checkout by placing items in a bin that reads all the tags at once."],
g:[["Tag","A small device with a chip and antenna"],["Reader","A device that sends and receives radio signals"],["NFC","Near-field communication"],["Contactless","Working without physical contact"]],
f:["Passive RFID tags have no battery.","NFC works within a few centimeters.","Pet microchips use RFID."],
fun:"Some RFID tags are so small that they can be embedded in paper, such as concert tickets and banknote experiments.",
tl:"Nag-iimbak ng impormasyon ang RFID tag na mababasa sa pamamagitan ng radio waves. Pinapayagan ng NFC, isang uri ng RFID, ang mga telepono at card na magbayad sa isang tapik, at ginagamit ang RFID sa paninda, alagang hayop, pasaporte at toll."});

ADDART({id:"printers",t:"Printers",c:"Technology",i:"🖨️",w:"Computer printer",r:["print","printing3d","comp","paper","typewriter"],
p:[["Paper from computers","Printers turn digital documents into marks on paper."],["Laser and inkjet","Most home and office printers use laser or inkjet technology."],["Ink costs","Printer ink is famously expensive."]],
l:"A computer printer is a device that produces text and images on paper or other materials from digital files. Early computer printers of the 1950s and 1960s were line printers and impact printers that struck an inked ribbon against paper, like typewriters; dot-matrix printers, which formed characters from tiny dots, were common in the 1970s and 1980s. Laser printing was invented at Xerox by Gary Starkweather in 1969, and the HP LaserJet of 1984 brought it to offices. Laser printers use a laser to draw an image on a drum with static electricity, attract powdered toner to it and fuse the toner onto paper with heat. Inkjet printers, which spray tiny droplets of ink, became affordable for homes in the late 1980s and 1990s and print color photos well. Printers are often sold cheaply while manufacturers profit from expensive ink cartridges, a ‘razor and blades’ business model that has drawn criticism and lawsuits. As documents went digital, printing declined in many offices, but printers remain common. 3D printers build solid objects layer by layer.",
s:[["Laser printers","A laser beam scans a rotating drum, changing the electric charge where text and images should appear. Toner particles stick to these areas, are transferred to paper and are melted on by a hot fuser, which is why freshly printed pages feel warm. Laser printers are fast and cheap per page for text."],["Inkjet printers","Thermal inkjets heat ink to form a bubble that pushes a droplet out, while piezoelectric inkjets use a crystal that flexes when electricity is applied. Print heads have hundreds of tiny nozzles. Inkjets are used for photos, posters and, in industry, for printing on fabric, packaging and even food."],["Ink prices","By volume, printer ink has been compared to expensive perfumes. Some printers block cheaper third-party cartridges using chips, and companies have faced lawsuits and fines over this. Refillable ink-tank printers have become popular as an alternative."],["Hidden dots","Many color laser printers add tiny, almost invisible yellow dots to pages, encoding the printer’s serial number and the date. This tracking, originally aimed at counterfeiters, was publicized by digital rights groups in the 2000s."]],
k:[["Main types","Laser and inkjet"],["Laser printer invented","Gary Starkweather, Xerox, 1969"],["Laser printer for offices","HP LaserJet, 1984"],["Older type","Dot-matrix"],["Business model","Cheap printers, costly ink"]],
d:[["1953","First high-speed printer for computers"],["1969","Laser printing invented at Xerox"],["1970s","Dot-matrix printers spread"],["1984","HP LaserJet launched"],["1988","Affordable inkjet printers for homes"],["2005","Hidden printer tracking dots publicized"]],
s2:[["The paperless office","Since the 1970s, people have predicted the ‘paperless office’. Paper use in offices actually rose for decades as printers made printing easy, before declining in the 2010s with email, cloud documents and smartphones."],["Printers and the Gutenberg legacy","Johannes Gutenberg’s printing press of the 1450s mass-produced books with movable type. Computer printers brought printing into homes, letting anyone publish documents."]],
sp:["PC LOAD LETTER","The confusing printer error message ‘PC LOAD LETTER’, meaning ‘add letter-size paper’, became a famous joke about baffling technology after it appeared in the 1999 comedy film Office Space."],
g:[["Toner","Powdered ink used in laser printers"],["Inkjet","A printer that sprays ink droplets"],["Dot-matrix","Printing using a grid of dots"],["Fuser","Part of a laser printer that melts toner onto paper"]],
f:["Laser printing was invented in 1969.","Freshly laser-printed pages are warm.","Some color printers add hidden tracking dots."],
fun:"Printer ink has been compared in price to vintage champagne, costing far more per liter than many luxury liquids.",
tl:"Ginagawang marka sa papel ng printer ang mga digital na dokumento. Laser o inkjet ang ginagamit ng karamihan ng printer sa bahay at opisina, at kilalang napakamahal ng tinta ng printer."});

ADDART({id:"vendingmachines",t:"Vending Machines",c:"Technology",i:"🥤",w:"Vending machine",r:["money","japancountry","atms","digitalpay","foodsafety"],
p:[["Automatic shops","Vending machines sell items automatically when money is inserted."],["Ancient origins","Hero of Alexandria described a coin-operated holy water dispenser."],["Japan’s favorite","Japan has about one vending machine for every 30 people."]],
l:"A vending machine is an automated machine that dispenses items such as snacks, drinks, tickets or other products after a customer inserts money or pays electronically. The earliest known vending machine was described by Hero of Alexandria in the 1st century AD: a coin dropped into a slot at an Egyptian temple pressed a lever that released a measured amount of holy water. Coin-operated machines selling postcards appeared in London in the 1880s, and in 1888 the Thomas Adams Gum Company installed machines selling chewing gum on New York’s elevated railway platforms. Vending machines spread through the 20th century, selling cigarettes, soft drinks, candy and coffee. Japan has one of the highest densities in the world, about 4 million machines, roughly one for every 30 people, selling hot and cold drinks, food, umbrellas and much more. Modern machines accept cards and phone payments, use touchscreens and track sales over the internet. Automated retail now includes machines selling fresh food, electronics and even cars.",
s:[["Hero’s machine","Hero’s holy water dispenser used a coin falling onto a pan attached to a lever. The weight tipped the pan, opening a valve until the coin slid off, so each coin gave the same amount of water. It is one of the earliest known automatic machines."],["Japan","Japan’s vending machines are popular because of low vandalism and crime, high labor costs and a culture of convenience. Many machines sell both hot and cold drinks from the same unit. Some act as emergency supplies, giving free drinks during earthquakes, and some carry Wi-Fi or AED defibrillators."],["How they work","Coin mechanisms check the weight, size and metal content of coins, and bill validators check banknotes with sensors. Spiral coils or robotic arms deliver products. Modern machines report stock and faults remotely so they can be restocked efficiently."],["Unusual machines","Vending machines around the world have sold live bait, fresh pizza made on the spot, books, gold bars, mashed potatoes and even cars, in multi-story ‘car vending’ towers in the United States."]],
k:[["Earliest known","Hero of Alexandria’s holy water dispenser"],["Modern start","1880s"],["Japan’s machines","About 4 million"],["Payment","Coins, notes, cards, phones"],["Unusual items","Pizza, gold, cars"]],
d:[["1st century AD","Hero describes a coin-operated dispenser"],["1883","Postcard vending machines in London"],["1888","Gum machines on New York train platforms"],["1926","Cigarette vending machines spread in the US"],["1960s","Vending machines spread rapidly in Japan"]],
s2:[["The Automat","Automats were restaurants where customers bought food from rows of small glass-door compartments by inserting coins. Horn & Hardart opened the first in Philadelphia in 1902, and they became famous in New York; the last closed in 1991."],["Vending machine dangers","Each year a few people are injured or killed by tipping vending machines over while trying to get stuck items or free products, leading to warning labels on many machines."]],
sp:["Disaster machines","After the 2011 earthquake and tsunami in Japan, some drink machines were programmed to give out free drinks during emergencies, and they display disaster information on electronic signs."],
g:[["Dispense","To give out"],["Bill validator","A device that checks banknotes"],["Automat","A restaurant using food vending compartments"],["Automated retail","Selling goods by machine"]],
f:["The first known vending machine dispensed holy water.","Japan has about 4 million vending machines.","Some vending machines sell cars."],
fun:"Some Japanese vending machines change their prices based on the outside temperature, and others recognize customers’ faces to suggest drinks.",
tl:"Awtomatikong nagbebenta ng mga paninda ang vending machine kapag hinulugan ng pera. Inilarawan ni Hero ng Alexandria ang isang makinang naglalabas ng banal na tubig, at may mga 4 milyong vending machine sa Japan."});

ADDART({id:"walkietalkies",t:"Walkie-Talkies and Two-Way Radios",c:"Technology",i:"📻",w:"Walkie-talkie",r:["radio","phone","ww2","firefighters","police"],
p:[["Push to talk","Walkie-talkies are handheld radios for talking over short distances."],["Born in wartime","They were developed for soldiers in World War II."],["Still vital","Police, firefighters and workers rely on two-way radios."]],
l:"A walkie-talkie is a portable, handheld two-way radio that lets people talk to each other over short distances without a phone network. Users press a ‘push-to-talk’ button to speak and release it to listen; usually only one person can talk at a time on a channel, which is why radio users say ‘over’ when they finish speaking. Several inventors developed portable two-way radios in the late 1930s, including Canadian Donald Hings and American Al Gross. During World War II, Motorola produced the backpack SCR-300, nicknamed the walkie-talkie, and the handheld SCR-536 ‘handie-talkie’ for soldiers. After the war, two-way radios spread to police, firefighters, taxis, construction sites, ships and aircraft, and simple walkie-talkies became popular toys. Today professional radio systems use digital technology and networks of repeaters for wider coverage, and emergency services rely on them because they work when phone networks fail. Citizens band (CB) and amateur, or ham, radio let members of the public communicate over longer distances.",
s:[["Radio etiquette","Radio users developed procedures to communicate clearly: ‘over’ means ‘I have finished; your turn’, ‘out’ means the conversation is over, ‘roger’ means ‘received’, and ‘wilco’ means ‘will comply’. The NATO phonetic alphabet, from Alfa to Zulu, spells words clearly."],["Inventors","Donald Hings built a portable radio for Canadian bush pilots and soldiers in 1937. Al Gross, a teenager in Ohio, built handheld radios in the late 1930s and later helped invent the pager and citizens band radio. Motorola engineers led by Dan Noble produced military radios in large numbers."],["Emergency communications","When disasters damage phone towers and power lines, two-way radios, repeaters and amateur radio operators keep communications going. Amateur radio volunteers provided vital links after hurricanes such as Katrina in 2005 and Maria in 2017."],["CB radio","Citizens band radio became a craze in the United States in the 1970s, especially among truckers, who used colorful slang such as ‘breaker one-nine’ and ‘smokey’ for police. The 1975 song ‘Convoy’ and films made CB culture famous."]],
k:[["Type","Handheld two-way radio"],["Key feature","Push-to-talk"],["Wartime models","SCR-300, SCR-536 (Motorola)"],["Inventors credited","Donald Hings, Al Gross"],["Users","Police, firefighters, workers, hobbyists"]],
d:[["1937","Donald Hings develops a portable radio"],["1938","Al Gross builds handheld radios"],["1940","Motorola’s SCR-536 handie-talkie"],["1958","Citizens band radio established in the US"],["1970s","CB radio craze in the US"]],
s2:[["Walkie-talkie or handie-talkie?","In World War II, ‘walkie-talkie’ referred to the larger backpack radio, while the handheld unit was a ‘handie-talkie’. After the war, ‘walkie-talkie’ came to mean the handheld type."],["Push-to-talk apps","Smartphone apps such as Zello offer push-to-talk features over the internet, and some phone networks provide push-to-talk services for businesses."]],
sp:["Mayday","The emergency distress call ‘Mayday’ was proposed in 1923 by radio officer Frederick Mockford at Croydon Airport in London, from the French m’aider, ‘help me’. It is repeated three times."],
g:[["Two-way radio","A radio that can send and receive"],["Push-to-talk","Pressing a button to transmit"],["Repeater","A station that rebroadcasts signals to extend range"],["Phonetic alphabet","Code words for letters"]],
f:["Radio users say ‘over’ when they finish speaking.","Walkie-talkies were developed for World War II.","Two-way radios work when phone networks fail."],
fun:"The word ‘roger’ comes from the old phonetic alphabet, in which R was ‘Roger’, standing for ‘received’.",
tl:"Ang walkie-talkie ay radyong hawak-kamay para makipag-usap sa maikling distansya. Nabuo ito para sa mga sundalo noong World War II, at umaasa pa rin dito ang pulis, bumbero at mga manggagawa."});

;
/* data/modern38.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 8): space.
ADDART({id:"europaclipper",t:"Europa Clipper",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Europa Clipper",r:["europa","jupiter","astrobio","enceladus","junoprobe"],
p:[["Mission to an ocean moon","Europa Clipper will study Jupiter’s moon Europa, which hides an ocean under its ice."],["NASA’s biggest planetary probe","It is the largest spacecraft NASA has built for a planetary mission."],["Arriving in 2030","After a six-year journey, it will reach Jupiter in April 2030."]],
l:"Europa Clipper is a NASA spacecraft on its way to study Europa, one of Jupiter’s four large moons, which scientists believe has a salty ocean of liquid water beneath its icy crust containing more water than all of Earth’s oceans. The mission’s goal is to determine whether Europa could support life by studying its ice shell, ocean, composition and geology. Launched on a SpaceX Falcon Heavy rocket on 14 October 2024, Europa Clipper is the largest spacecraft NASA has ever built for a planetary mission, with solar arrays spanning about 30 meters to collect faint sunlight at Jupiter. It flew past Mars in March 2025 and is due to swing past Earth in December 2026 to gain speed before arriving at Jupiter in April 2030. Rather than orbiting Europa, which lies in Jupiter’s intense radiation belts, it will orbit Jupiter and make about 49 close flybys of Europa, some as low as 25 kilometers above the surface, using nine science instruments including cameras, ice-penetrating radar and a mass spectrometer to sample particles. The European Space Agency’s Juice spacecraft will also study Jupiter’s icy moons at the same time.",
s:[["Why Europa?","Europa’s surface is crisscrossed with cracks and ridges, suggesting moving ice over an ocean. Jupiter’s gravity flexes the moon, heating its interior. Water, chemical ingredients and energy, three needs for life as we know it, may all be present. The Hubble Space Telescope has seen possible plumes of water vapor."],["Surviving radiation","Jupiter’s radiation could fry electronics. Clipper’s sensitive electronics are protected inside a vault with walls of titanium and aluminum. Each flyby is quick, and the spacecraft spends most of its time farther from Jupiter."],["Instruments","Instruments include cameras to map the surface, a thermal imager to find warm spots, radar to probe the ice shell for water pockets, a magnetometer to measure the ocean’s depth and saltiness, and dust and gas analyzers to sample particles knocked off the surface or possible plumes."],["A message on board","The spacecraft carries a plate engraved with ‘In Praise of Mystery: A Poem for Europa’ by US Poet Laureate Ada Limón, along with the names of more than 2.6 million people and the word ‘water’ in 103 languages."]],
k:[["Target","Jupiter’s moon Europa"],["Launch","14 October 2024 (Falcon Heavy)"],["Arrival at Jupiter","April 2030"],["Planned flybys","About 49"],["Solar array span","About 30 m"]],
d:[["1979","Voyager images reveal Europa’s cracked ice"],["1995–2003","Galileo spacecraft finds evidence of an ocean"],["2015","NASA approves Europa Clipper"],["2024","Europa Clipper launches"],["2025","Mars flyby"],["2026","Earth flyby (December)"],["2030","Arrival at Jupiter"]],
s2:[["Juice","ESA’s Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer, Juice, launched in April 2023, will arrive at Jupiter in 2031 and eventually orbit Ganymede, the largest moon in the solar system. Scientists plan to coordinate observations between the two missions."],["Could we find life?","Europa Clipper cannot detect life directly, but it will look for conditions that could support it. A future lander might search for signs of life in Europa’s ice."]],
sp:["A huge spacecraft","With its solar arrays unfolded, Europa Clipper is about as wide as a basketball court. It weighed about 6,000 kilograms at launch, more than half of it propellant."],
g:[["Flyby","Passing close to an object without orbiting it"],["Ice shell","The frozen outer layer of an icy moon"],["Plume","A jet of gas or vapor"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change speed"]],
f:["Europa may have more water than Earth’s oceans.","Europa Clipper will fly past Europa about 49 times.","It carries a poem for Europa."],
fun:"Europa Clipper’s ice-penetrating radar may be able to see up to about 30 kilometers into Europa’s icy crust.",
tl:"Pag-aaralan ng Europa Clipper ang Europa, buwan ng Jupiter na may karagatan sa ilalim ng yelo. Ito ang pinakamalaking spacecraft na ginawa ng NASA para sa misyon sa planeta, at darating ito sa Jupiter sa Abril 2030."});

ADDART({id:"psyche",t:"Psyche: The Metal Asteroid",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⚙️",w:"Psyche (spacecraft)",r:["asteroidbelt","ionengines","vesta","pulsar","mars"],
p:[["A metal world","The asteroid Psyche may be the exposed core of an early planet."],["NASA’s Psyche mission","A NASA spacecraft is traveling there, arriving in 2029."],["Lasers in space","Psyche tested laser communication across deep space."]],
l:"Psyche is both a large, unusual asteroid and the NASA spacecraft sent to explore it. The asteroid 16 Psyche, discovered by Italian astronomer Annibale de Gasparis on 17 March 1852, orbits in the outer asteroid belt and is about 280 kilometers across at its widest. Observations from Earth suggest that it contains a large amount of metal, iron and nickel, perhaps mixed with rock, leading scientists to suspect it could be part of the core of a planetesimal, an early building block of planets, whose outer layers were stripped away by collisions. Because no one can visit Earth’s core, Psyche offers a unique chance to study what planetary cores are like. The Psyche spacecraft launched on a Falcon Heavy rocket on 13 October 2023 and uses solar-powered Hall-effect thrusters. It flew past Mars on 15 May 2026, gaining speed, and is due to arrive at Psyche in August 2029 to orbit for about two years, mapping its surface, magnetic field and composition. The spacecraft also carried an experiment that sent laser signals from deep space to Earth.",
s:[["What is Psyche made of?","Radar and telescope observations show Psyche is denser and more reflective in radar than most asteroids, suggesting metal. Its density, however, suggests it may also contain rock or be porous. Scientists will find out only when the spacecraft arrives."],["The spacecraft","About the size of a small van with solar arrays spanning about 25 meters, Psyche carries a multispectral imager, a gamma-ray and neutron spectrometer to measure its elements, and a magnetometer to detect any ancient magnetic field, which would support the core theory."],["Laser communication","Psyche carried NASA’s Deep Space Optical Communications experiment, which in 2023 and 2024 beamed high-definition video and data to Earth by laser from millions of kilometers away, including a video of a cat named Taters, showing that lasers could greatly increase data rates for future missions."],["The ‘quadrillion-dollar’ myth","News stories often claim that Psyche’s metals are worth quadrillions of dollars. Scientists say such figures are meaningless, as there is no way to mine or bring the metal back, and flooding markets would make it worthless."]],
k:[["Asteroid size","About 280 km across"],["Discovered","17 March 1852"],["Launch","13 October 2023"],["Mars flyby","15 May 2026"],["Arrival","August 2029"]],
d:[["1852","Annibale de Gasparis discovers Psyche"],["2017","NASA selects the Psyche mission"],["2022","Launch delayed by software problems"],["2023","Psyche launches"],["2026","Mars gravity assist"],["2029","Planned arrival at Psyche"]],
s2:[["A delayed launch","Psyche was meant to launch in 2022, but problems testing its flight software caused a delay. An independent review found management and staffing problems at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory, leading to changes."],["Named after a myth","The asteroid is named after Psyche, a figure in Greek mythology who represents the soul and married Eros, the god of love."]],
sp:["Lindy Elkins-Tanton","Planetary scientist Lindy Elkins-Tanton of Arizona State University leads the Psyche mission, one of the few NASA planetary missions led by a woman."],
g:[["Planetesimal","An early building block of planets"],["Core","The central part of a planet"],["Hall-effect thruster","A type of electric engine"],["Spectrometer","An instrument that analyzes light or radiation"]],
f:["Psyche may be part of an ancient planet’s core.","The spacecraft flew past Mars in May 2026.","Psyche beamed a cat video to Earth by laser."],
fun:"The first high-definition video sent by laser from deep space showed a cat named Taters chasing a laser pointer.",
tl:"Ang Psyche ay asteroid na maaaring ang nakalantad na core ng isang sinaunang planeta. Naglalakbay doon ang isang spacecraft ng NASA na darating sa 2029, at sumubok ito ng komunikasyon gamit ang laser sa malalim na kalawakan."});

ADDART({id:"hayabusa",t:"Hayabusa and Hayabusa2",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🦅",w:"Hayabusa2",r:["asteroidbelt","ionengines","japancountry","spaceagencies","osirisrex"],
p:[["Japan’s asteroid samplers","Japan’s Hayabusa probes brought pieces of asteroids back to Earth."],["A dramatic comeback","The first Hayabusa returned in 2010 after many problems."],["Ryugu samples","Hayabusa2 returned samples from asteroid Ryugu in 2020."]],
l:"Hayabusa and Hayabusa2, named after the Japanese word for peregrine falcon, are robotic spacecraft of the Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency (JAXA) that made the first sample returns from asteroids. Hayabusa launched in 2003 and reached the small asteroid Itokawa in 2005. Despite a failed mini-lander, broken reaction wheels, a fuel leak, loss of contact for weeks and ion engine failures, engineers nursed it home; on 13 June 2010 its capsule landed in Australia with about 1,500 tiny grains of Itokawa, the first asteroid material ever returned. Hayabusa2 launched on 3 December 2014 and reached the carbon-rich asteroid Ryugu in June 2018. It released small hopping rovers, fired a copper projectile to blast a crater and touched down twice to collect samples, returning a capsule to Australia on 6 December 2020 with 5.4 grams of material, including organic molecules, amino acids and uracil, a component of RNA. Hayabusa2 continued on an extended mission, flying past the asteroid Torifune in July 2026 and heading for 1998 KY26 in 2031.",
s:[["Hayabusa’s troubles","After landing on Itokawa in 2005, Hayabusa’s thrusters leaked, it tumbled and contact was lost for seven weeks. Engineers recovered it and, when its ion engines failed, cross-wired two engines to make one working combination. The spacecraft burned up on reentry as planned while its capsule parachuted down."],["Hayabusa2 at Ryugu","Ryugu turned out to be a diamond-shaped rubble pile covered with boulders, making landing difficult. In 2019, Hayabusa2 fired a 2-kilogram copper impactor to create an artificial crater and later collected material from beneath the surface, the first subsurface sample from an asteroid."],["What the samples showed","Ryugu samples are among the most primitive materials studied, similar to rare meteorites. They contain water-bearing minerals and organic compounds, supporting the idea that asteroids may have delivered water and ingredients for life to the early Earth."],["The extended mission","After dropping off its capsule, Hayabusa2 headed out again. It flew past asteroid 2001 CC21, named Torifune, in July 2026 and is due to rendezvous with 1998 KY26, a tiny, fast-spinning asteroid, in 2031."]],
k:[["Agency","JAXA"],["Hayabusa target","Itokawa (returned 2010)"],["Hayabusa2 target","Ryugu (returned 2020)"],["Ryugu sample","5.4 grams"],["Next target","1998 KY26 (2031)"]],
d:[["2003","Hayabusa launches"],["2005","Hayabusa reaches Itokawa"],["2010","Hayabusa returns Itokawa grains"],["2014","Hayabusa2 launches"],["2019","Hayabusa2 blasts a crater on Ryugu"],["2020","Ryugu samples land in Australia"],["2026","Hayabusa2 flies past Torifune"]],
s2:[["A national hero","The first Hayabusa’s survival made it a celebrity in Japan, inspiring several feature films and a planetarium show. People saw its return as a story of perseverance."],["Sharing samples","JAXA shared Ryugu samples with NASA, which in return gave JAXA part of the asteroid Bennu sample from OSIRIS-REx."]],
sp:["MINERVA rovers","Hayabusa2 deployed two tiny MINERVA-II1 rovers on Ryugu in 2018, the first rovers to move on an asteroid. Because gravity was so weak, they moved by hopping."],
g:[["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"],["Rubble pile","An asteroid made of loose rocks held by gravity"],["Organic molecule","A carbon-based molecule"],["Reaction wheel","A wheel used to point a spacecraft"]],
f:["Hayabusa made the first asteroid sample return.","Hayabusa2 blasted a crater on Ryugu.","Ryugu samples contain amino acids."],
fun:"The tiny MINERVA rovers on Ryugu moved by hopping, because a single step could have launched them off into space in its weak gravity.",
tl:"Nagdala ng mga piraso ng asteroid sa Earth ang mga Hayabusa probe ng Japan. Bumalik ang unang Hayabusa noong 2010 sa kabila ng maraming problema, at nagdala ang Hayabusa2 ng mga sample mula sa asteroid na Ryugu noong 2020."});

ADDART({id:"osirisrex",t:"OSIRIS-REx",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☄️",w:"OSIRIS-REx",r:["asteroidbelt","hayabusa","planetdefense","astrobio","dna"],
p:[["Bringing back Bennu","OSIRIS-REx collected a sample from asteroid Bennu and returned it in 2023."],["Ingredients for life","The sample contains amino acids and all five bases of DNA and RNA."],["Off to Apophis","Renamed OSIRIS-APEX, it will visit asteroid Apophis in 2029."]],
l:"OSIRIS-REx was a NASA mission that collected a sample from the near-Earth asteroid Bennu and returned it to Earth, the first US asteroid sample return. Its name stands for Origins, Spectral Interpretation, Resource Identification and Security–Regolith Explorer. Launched on 8 September 2016, it reached Bennu, a carbon-rich rubble-pile asteroid about 500 meters wide, in December 2018 and mapped it in detail. On 20 October 2020, the spacecraft briefly touched the surface with its robotic arm in a ‘touch-and-go’ maneuver, firing nitrogen gas to stir up rocks and dust; it sank surprisingly deep, revealing that Bennu’s surface is loosely packed like a ball pit. The sample capsule landed in the Utah desert on 24 September 2023 with about 121.6 grams of material, the largest asteroid sample ever returned. Studies published in 2025 found that it contains 14 of the 20 amino acids used by life on Earth, all five nucleobases found in DNA and RNA, and salts left by evaporating brine. The spacecraft, renamed OSIRIS-APEX, is heading for the asteroid Apophis, arriving in 2029.",
s:[["Bennu","Bennu is about 4.5 billion years old and may contain material from the birth of the solar system. It also has a small chance, about 1 in 2,700, of hitting Earth in the late 2100s, which made studying it useful for planetary defense. OSIRIS-REx precisely measured its orbit."],["Touch-and-go","During the sampling maneuver, the arm’s head pushed about half a meter into Bennu’s surface, collecting so much material that some leaked out through a flap jammed open by rocks. Engineers quickly stowed the sample to avoid losing more."],["Opening the capsule","Scientists at NASA’s Johnson Space Center took months to open the sample container fully, because two stubborn fasteners would not unscrew until special tools were made. Samples are shared with researchers around the world."],["Findings","The Bennu sample shows that its parent body once had liquid salty water and contained many ingredients important for life. Scientists stress that it contains no signs of life itself, but supports the idea that asteroids delivered these ingredients to early Earth."]],
k:[["Target","Asteroid Bennu"],["Launch","8 September 2016"],["Sample collected","20 October 2020"],["Sample landed","24 September 2023, Utah"],["Sample mass","About 121.6 g"]],
d:[["2016","OSIRIS-REx launches"],["2018","Arrives at Bennu"],["2020","Collects its sample"],["2023","Sample capsule lands in Utah"],["2025","Studies report amino acids and nucleobases"],["2029","OSIRIS-APEX to reach Apophis"]],
s2:[["OSIRIS-APEX","OSIRIS-APEX will reach Apophis shortly after the asteroid’s close pass of Earth on 13 April 2029, studying how Earth’s gravity changed its surface and spin."],["A Japanese partner","OSIRIS-REx and Japan’s Hayabusa2 studied similar carbon-rich asteroids at the same time, and the two agencies exchanged portions of their samples."]],
sp:["Dante Lauretta","Planetary scientist Dante Lauretta of the University of Arizona led OSIRIS-REx for nearly two decades and later wrote a book about the mission, The Asteroid Hunter."],
g:[["Regolith","Loose material covering a solid surface"],["Nucleobase","A building block of DNA and RNA"],["Touch-and-go","A brief contact to collect a sample"],["Brine","Very salty water"]],
f:["OSIRIS-REx returned 121.6 grams from Bennu.","The Bennu sample contains all five DNA and RNA bases.","The spacecraft will visit Apophis in 2029."],
fun:"Bennu’s surface turned out to be so loose that the spacecraft would have sunk into it if it had not fired its thrusters to back away.",
tl:"Kumuha ang OSIRIS-REx ng sample mula sa asteroid na Bennu at ibinalik ito noong 2023. Naglalaman ang sample ng mga amino acid at lahat ng limang base ng DNA at RNA, at pupunta ang spacecraft sa asteroid na Apophis sa 2029."});

ADDART({id:"gaia",t:"Gaia Space Observatory",c:"Space Exploration",i:"✨",w:"Gaia (spacecraft)",r:["milky","lagrange","distances","stars","exoplanet"],
p:[["Mapping the galaxy","Gaia made the most precise map of the Milky Way ever."],["Nearly 2 billion stars","It measured positions, distances and motions of about 1.8 billion stars."],["Retired in 2025","Gaia stopped observing in January 2025 and was switched off in March."]],
l:"Gaia was a European Space Agency space observatory that created the largest and most precise three-dimensional map of the Milky Way. Launched on 19 December 2013 and operating from the Sun–Earth L2 Lagrange point about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth, Gaia scanned the whole sky repeatedly, measuring the positions, distances, motions, brightness and colors of nearly 2 billion stars, about 1 percent of the stars in our galaxy. It measured distances using parallax, with accuracy equivalent to measuring the width of a human hair from about 1,000 kilometers away. Gaia’s data releases, especially the third in June 2022, transformed astronomy: they revealed the history of the Milky Way, including ancient mergers with smaller galaxies; mapped how stars move; found hundreds of thousands of asteroids, binary stars and variable stars; and detected dormant black holes. Gaia stopped collecting data on 15 January 2025 and was switched off on 27 March 2025. Its fourth data release, covering the first five and a half years of observations, is planned for 2 December 2026, with a final release around 2030.",
s:[["How Gaia worked","Gaia had two telescopes pointing in different directions, feeding light onto a camera of 106 electronic sensors with nearly a billion pixels, the largest ever sent into space at the time. As the spacecraft spun slowly, stars drifted across the sensors, and their positions were measured many times over years."],["Galactic archaeology","Gaia found that about 8 to 11 billion years ago, the Milky Way swallowed a smaller galaxy, nicknamed Gaia–Enceladus, whose stars still move differently from others. It also showed that the Milky Way’s disk is warped and rippling, possibly from interactions with the Sagittarius dwarf galaxy."],["Black holes","In 2024, astronomers using Gaia data found Gaia BH3, a dormant black hole about 33 times the mass of the Sun only about 1,900 light-years away, the most massive stellar black hole known in our galaxy, detected by the wobble of its companion star."],["Data releases","Gaia’s data are freely available. Data Release 3 included about 1.8 billion sources. Data Release 4, due on 2 December 2026, will include time-series measurements and is expected to reveal thousands of exoplanets through their stars’ wobbles."]],
k:[["Agency","European Space Agency"],["Launch","19 December 2013"],["Location","Sun–Earth L2"],["Stars measured","About 1.8 billion"],["Retired","27 March 2025"]],
d:[["2013","Gaia launches"],["2016","First data release"],["2018","Second data release"],["2022","Third data release"],["2025","Gaia stops observing and is switched off"],["2026","Fourth data release planned (2 December)"]],
s2:[["A gentle goodbye","Before switching Gaia off, controllers moved it away from L2 into a stable orbit around the Sun so it would not interfere with other telescopes, and the team uploaded a farewell message including the names of team members."],["Hipparcos","Gaia succeeded ESA’s Hipparcos mission (1989–1993), the first space mission to measure star positions precisely, which mapped about 118,000 stars. Gaia measured more than 10,000 times as many."]],
sp:["Gaia and asteroids","Although designed to study stars, Gaia also measured the orbits of hundreds of thousands of asteroids in our solar system with great precision, helping planetary defense."],
g:[["Parallax","The apparent shift of a star seen from different places"],["Astrometry","Measuring the positions and motions of stars"],["Data release","A published set of mission data"],["Dormant black hole","A black hole not actively feeding"]],
f:["Gaia mapped about 1.8 billion stars.","Gaia was switched off in March 2025.","Its fourth data release is planned for December 2026."],
fun:"Gaia’s measurements were so precise that it could detect the motion of stars equivalent to the growth of a human hair seen from across a continent.",
tl:"Gumawa ang Gaia ng pinakatumpak na mapa ng Milky Way. Sinukat nito ang posisyon, layo at galaw ng mga 1.8 bilyong bituin, at itinigil ang operasyon nito noong 2025."});

ADDART({id:"chandra",t:"Chandra X-ray Observatory",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Chandra X-ray Observatory",r:["scope","bh","supernova","hubblest","spaceshuttle"],
p:[["X-ray eyes","Chandra observes the universe in X-rays, which Earth’s atmosphere blocks."],["Launched by the Shuttle","It was launched in 1999 on a mission commanded by Eileen Collins."],["Fighting budget cuts","Its future has been threatened by NASA budget cuts."]],
l:"The Chandra X-ray Observatory is a NASA space telescope that detects X-rays from very hot and energetic objects in the universe, such as black holes, exploding stars, neutron stars and galaxy clusters. Because Earth’s atmosphere absorbs X-rays, such telescopes must operate in space. Chandra was launched on 23 July 1999 aboard the Space Shuttle Columbia on a mission commanded by Eileen Collins, the first woman to command a Space Shuttle flight. It travels in a long, elliptical orbit reaching about a third of the way to the Moon. Named after Indian-American astrophysicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, who won the 1983 Nobel Prize in Physics for his work on how stars die, Chandra is one of NASA’s four ‘Great Observatories’, along with Hubble, the Compton Gamma Ray Observatory and Spitzer. Its mirrors are the smoothest ever made, allowing exceptionally sharp X-ray images. Designed for five years, Chandra has operated for more than 27 years. Proposed NASA budget cuts since 2024 have threatened it; Congress provided at least 63 million dollars for 2026, and astronomers continue to campaign to keep it running.",
s:[["How X-ray telescopes work","X-rays pass through ordinary mirrors, so Chandra uses nested, barrel-shaped mirrors that X-rays strike at shallow angles, like stones skipping on water. The mirrors are polished so smooth that, if the state of Colorado were as smooth, Pikes Peak would be less than an inch tall."],["Discoveries","Chandra has imaged the remains of exploding stars such as Cassiopeia A, discovered powerful jets and outbursts from supermassive black holes, studied the Milky Way’s central black hole, Sagittarius A*, and helped provide evidence for dark matter by observing the Bullet Cluster, where colliding galaxy clusters separated hot gas from mass."],["Images","Chandra images are often combined with images from Hubble, Webb and other telescopes, assigning colors to X-ray data, to create striking pictures of nebulae and galaxies. In 2020, NASA released ‘sonifications’ turning Chandra data into sound."],["Budget threats","In 2024, NASA proposed sharply reducing Chandra’s budget, which scientists warned could end the mission. A 2026 budget line kept it operating at reduced levels. One of its instruments has had problems since October 2025, but Chandra otherwise works normally."]],
k:[["Observes","X-rays"],["Launch","23 July 1999 (Space Shuttle Columbia)"],["Named after","Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar"],["Orbit","Elliptical, up to about a third of the way to the Moon"],["Design life","5 years (now more than 27)"]],
d:[["1976","Chandra proposed to NASA"],["1983","Chandrasekhar wins the Nobel Prize"],["1999","Chandra launched on Columbia"],["2006","Bullet Cluster observations support dark matter"],["2024","Proposed budget cuts threaten Chandra"],["2026","Congress funds Chandra at reduced levels"]],
s2:[["Eileen Collins","Eileen Collins, a US Air Force pilot, became the first woman to pilot a Space Shuttle in 1995 and the first to command one on the Chandra launch mission in 1999. She later commanded the first shuttle flight after the Columbia disaster in 2005."],["The Great Observatories","NASA’s four Great Observatories each studied different kinds of light: Hubble (visible and ultraviolet), Compton (gamma rays), Chandra (X-rays) and Spitzer (infrared). Only Hubble and Chandra still operate."]],
sp:["Chandrasekhar","Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar calculated as a young man in 1930 that a white dwarf star more than about 1.4 times the Sun’s mass cannot support itself and must collapse, a result called the Chandrasekhar limit. His name means ‘moon-holder’ in Sanskrit."],
g:[["X-ray","High-energy light invisible to the eye"],["Great Observatories","NASA’s four large space telescopes"],["Galaxy cluster","A group of hundreds or thousands of galaxies"],["Sonification","Turning data into sound"]],
f:["Earth’s atmosphere blocks X-rays.","Chandra’s mirrors are the smoothest ever made.","Chandra has worked for more than 27 years."],
fun:"Chandra is so sensitive that it could read a stop sign from about 20 kilometers away, in X-ray terms.",
tl:"Inoobserbahan ng Chandra ang uniberso gamit ang X-ray, na hinaharangan ng atmospera ng Earth. Inilunsad ito noong 1999 sa misyong pinamunuan ni Eileen Collins, at nanganib ang hinaharap nito dahil sa pagbawas sa badyet ng NASA."});

ADDART({id:"skylab",t:"Skylab",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Skylab",r:["iss","mir","saturnv","spacewalks","apollo"],
p:[["America’s first space station","Skylab was the first US space station, launched in 1973."],["Saved by its crew","Astronauts repaired serious damage from its launch."],["A fiery end","Skylab fell back to Earth over Australia in 1979."]],
l:"Skylab was the first space station of the United States, launched on 14 May 1973 on the last flight of a Saturn V rocket. Built from the converted third stage of a Saturn V, it was the largest spacecraft of its time, with a workshop, living quarters, a solar observatory called the Apollo Telescope Mount and an airlock. Skylab was damaged during launch when its micrometeoroid shield and one main solar panel tore away, causing it to overheat. The first crew, launched on 25 May 1973, deployed a parasol-like sunshade and freed the remaining stuck solar panel during spacewalks, saving the station. Three crews of three astronauts lived aboard for 28, 59 and 84 days between 1973 and 1974, conducting medical experiments on long-term weightlessness, observing the Sun, studying Earth and testing living in space. The final crew’s 84-day mission was a US record for 20 years. Skylab was left empty, and plans to boost it with the Space Shuttle came too late; on 11 July 1979 it reentered the atmosphere, scattering debris across the Indian Ocean and Western Australia.",
s:[["Living in space","Skylab had a shower, a dining table, sleeping compartments and an exercise bicycle. Astronauts found that they grew taller in weightlessness and lost muscle and bone. They ran around the inside of a large tank-shaped room for exercise and fun."],["Solar science","The Apollo Telescope Mount took more than 150,000 images of the Sun in ultraviolet and X-rays, revealing coronal holes, regions where the solar wind streams out, and recording solar flares in unprecedented detail."],["The ‘mutiny’","The third crew complained of an exhausting schedule and took an unscheduled rest day in December 1973, sometimes called a strike or mutiny. NASA adjusted their schedule, and the episode taught planners about workloads on long missions."],["Skylab falls","As Skylab’s orbit decayed faster than expected because of high solar activity, the world watched its reentry. Debris fell near Esperance, Western Australia. The local council jokingly fined NASA 400 Australian dollars for littering; a US radio host paid the fine in 2009."]],
k:[["Launch","14 May 1973"],["Crews","Three (1973–1974)"],["Longest mission","84 days"],["Built from","Saturn V third stage"],["Reentry","11 July 1979"]],
d:[["1973","Skylab launches and is damaged"],["1973","First crew repairs the station"],["1974","Third crew completes an 84-day mission"],["1979","Skylab falls to Earth over Australia"],["2009","Esperance’s littering fine is paid"]],
s2:[["Spiders in space","Two spiders, Arabella and Anita, flew on Skylab as part of a student experiment. At first they spun messy webs in weightlessness but soon adapted and spun more normal webs."],["Skylab’s legacy","Skylab’s experiments on health, exercise and living in space informed later stations such as Mir and the International Space Station."]],
sp:["Comet Kohoutek","The third Skylab crew observed Comet Kohoutek in late 1973, which had been predicted to be spectacular but proved disappointing from the ground. The astronauts saw it well from space."],
g:[["Space station","A spacecraft where crews live for long periods"],["Micrometeoroid","A tiny particle of space rock"],["Reentry","Returning into Earth’s atmosphere"],["Coronal hole","A dark region of the Sun’s corona"]],
f:["Skylab was America’s first space station.","Astronauts repaired Skylab with a sunshade.","Skylab fell to Earth over Australia in 1979."],
fun:"A teenager in Esperance found pieces of Skylab and flew to San Francisco to claim a 10,000-dollar prize offered by a newspaper for the first piece delivered.",
tl:"Ang Skylab ang unang istasyong pangkalawakan ng Estados Unidos, inilunsad noong 1973. Inayos ng mga astronaut ang malubhang pinsala nito, at bumagsak ito sa Earth sa ibabaw ng Australia noong 1979."});

ADDART({id:"projectmercury",t:"Project Mercury and Gemini",c:"Space Exploration",i:"👨‍🚀",w:"Project Mercury",r:["space","apollo","gagarin","spacewalks","astronauts"],
p:[["America’s first astronauts","Project Mercury put the first Americans into space."],["The Mercury Seven","NASA’s first seven astronauts became national heroes."],["Practicing for the Moon","Gemini tested spacewalks, docking and long flights for Apollo."]],
l:"Project Mercury (1958–1963) was the first human spaceflight program of the United States, and Project Gemini (1961–1966) was its successor, preparing for the Apollo Moon landings. NASA, founded in 1958, chose seven military test pilots as its first astronauts, the ‘Mercury Seven’, introduced in April 1959. Mercury’s one-person capsules launched on Redstone and Atlas rockets. On 5 May 1961, less than a month after Yuri Gagarin’s orbital flight, Alan Shepard became the first American in space on a 15-minute suborbital flight, and on 20 February 1962, John Glenn became the first American to orbit Earth, circling it three times. Mercury flew six crewed missions. Gemini used larger two-person capsules launched on Titan rockets and flew ten crewed missions in 1965 and 1966. Gemini astronauts made the first US spacewalk, stayed in space for up to two weeks, carried out the first rendezvous between two spacecraft and the first docking, and practiced the skills needed for lunar missions. Gemini 8, in March 1966, nearly ended in disaster when a stuck thruster sent the spacecraft spinning, but Neil Armstrong regained control.",
s:[["The Mercury Seven","The Mercury Seven were Scott Carpenter, Gordon Cooper, John Glenn, Gus Grissom, Wally Schirra, Alan Shepard and Deke Slayton. Their story was told in Tom Wolfe’s book The Right Stuff (1979) and its 1983 film."],["Glenn’s flight","During John Glenn’s Friendship 7 flight, a sensor suggested his heat shield might be loose. Controllers told him to keep his retrorocket pack on during reentry, and he returned safely. In 1998, at age 77, Glenn flew again on the Space Shuttle, becoming the oldest person in space at that time."],["Gemini’s achievements","Ed White made the first American spacewalk on Gemini 4 in June 1965. Gemini 7 spent nearly 14 days in space, and Gemini 6A rendezvoused with it in December 1965. Gemini 8 achieved the first docking in orbit with an uncrewed Agena target vehicle."],["Hidden Figures","African American mathematicians at NASA, including Katherine Johnson, calculated trajectories for Mercury flights. Glenn reportedly asked for Johnson to check the computer’s calculations before his flight. Their story was told in the 2016 book and film Hidden Figures."]],
k:[["Mercury years","1958–1963"],["Gemini years","1961–1966 (flights 1965–1966)"],["First American in space","Alan Shepard, 5 May 1961"],["First American in orbit","John Glenn, 20 February 1962"],["First docking","Gemini 8, 16 March 1966"]],
d:[["1958","NASA founded; Project Mercury begins"],["1959","Mercury Seven introduced"],["1961","Alan Shepard flies into space"],["1962","John Glenn orbits Earth"],["1965","First US spacewalk on Gemini 4"],["1966","First docking on Gemini 8"]],
s2:[["Ham the chimpanzee","Before Shepard’s flight, a chimpanzee named Ham flew a Mercury capsule on 31 January 1961, pulling levers in response to lights, showing that tasks could be performed in spaceflight."],["Liberty Bell 7","Gus Grissom’s capsule, Liberty Bell 7, sank after splashdown in July 1961 when its hatch blew open early. Grissom nearly drowned. The capsule was recovered from the Atlantic seabed in 1999."]],
sp:["Gemini’s name","Gemini, Latin for ‘twins’, was named after the constellation because each capsule carried two astronauts."],
g:[["Suborbital","Reaching space without completing an orbit"],["Rendezvous","Two spacecraft meeting in orbit"],["Docking","Joining two spacecraft"],["Retrorocket","A rocket fired to slow down"]],
f:["Alan Shepard was the first American in space.","John Glenn orbited Earth in 1962.","Gemini made the first docking in space."],
fun:"Alan Shepard’s first flight lasted only about 15 minutes, while John Glenn flew in space again 36 years after his first orbital flight.",
tl:"Inilagay ng Project Mercury ang mga unang Amerikano sa kalawakan, at naging pambansang bayani ang Mercury Seven. Sinubukan naman ng Gemini ang spacewalk, docking at mahahabang paglipad bilang paghahanda sa Apollo."});

ADDART({id:"soyuz",t:"Soyuz",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Soyuz (spacecraft)",r:["iss","sputnik","spaceports","gagarin","mir"],
p:[["The space workhorse","Soyuz spacecraft have carried people to space since 1967."],["Longest-serving","Soyuz is the longest-used crewed spacecraft in history."],["A lifeline to the ISS","For years it was the only way to reach the International Space Station."]],
l:"Soyuz, meaning ‘union’ in Russian, is a series of spacecraft designed by the Soviet Union in the 1960s and still used by Russia to carry cosmonauts and astronauts to space, making it the longest-serving crewed spacecraft in history. The name also refers to the family of rockets that launch it, descended from the R-7 missile that launched Sputnik in 1957. A Soyuz spacecraft has three parts: a spherical orbital module for living space, a bell-shaped descent module in which the crew of up to three return to Earth, and a service module with engines and solar panels. The first crewed flight, Soyuz 1, ended in tragedy in April 1967 when its parachute failed and cosmonaut Vladimir Komarov was killed; in 1971 the three-man Soyuz 11 crew died when their capsule lost air during return. Since then, Soyuz has become known for reliability. After the Space Shuttle retired in 2011, Soyuz was the only way to carry people to the International Space Station until SpaceX’s Crew Dragon began flights in 2020. Soyuz launches from Baikonur, Kazakhstan, where a launch pad damaged in November 2025 was repaired in March 2026.",
s:[["The spacecraft","The descent module lands on land in the steppes of Kazakhstan under a large parachute, firing small rockets about a meter above the ground to soften the landing. Crews describe the landing as a hard jolt. The other modules burn up in the atmosphere."],["Soyuz and the ISS","Soyuz spacecraft dock with the ISS and remain attached as lifeboats during crews’ stays of about six months. Under a seat-swap agreement, American astronauts fly on Soyuz and Russian cosmonauts on US commercial spacecraft."],["Emergencies","On 11 October 2018, the Soyuz MS-10 rocket failed shortly after launch. The launch escape system pulled the capsule away, and astronauts Aleksey Ovchinin and Nick Hague landed safely. Both flew again months later. In 1983, Soyuz T-10-1 crew escaped seconds before their rocket exploded on the pad."],["Baikonur pad damage","After Soyuz MS-28 launched on 27 November 2025, a service platform collapsed into the flame trench at Baikonur’s Site 31, the only pad able to launch crews to the ISS. Russia repaired the pad, and launches resumed in March 2026."]],
k:[["Meaning","‘Union’"],["First crewed flight","Soyuz 1, April 1967"],["Crew","Up to three"],["Landing","On land in Kazakhstan"],["Launch site","Baikonur Cosmodrome"]],
d:[["1967","Soyuz 1 disaster kills Vladimir Komarov"],["1971","Soyuz 11 crew dies during return"],["1975","Apollo–Soyuz docking in orbit"],["2011","Soyuz becomes the only crew vehicle to the ISS"],["2018","Soyuz MS-10 crew survives a launch failure"],["2025","Launch pad damaged after Soyuz MS-28"],["2026","Pad repaired; launches resume"]],
s2:[["Apollo–Soyuz","In July 1975, an American Apollo spacecraft docked with Soyuz 19 in orbit, and the crews shook hands in space, a symbol of détente during the Cold War and a forerunner of later cooperation on the ISS."],["Space tourists","Between 2001 and 2009, seven private space tourists, including Dennis Tito, flew to the ISS aboard Soyuz, paying tens of millions of dollars each."]],
sp:["Traditions","Before launch, Soyuz crews follow traditions, such as watching the film White Sun of the Desert, signing their hotel room doors and planting trees at Baikonur. A small toy hangs in the capsule as a zero-gravity indicator."],
g:[["Descent module","The capsule that returns the crew to Earth"],["Launch escape system","A rocket that pulls the capsule away in an emergency"],["Steppe","A large, flat grassland"],["Détente","An easing of tension between countries"]],
f:["Soyuz has flown people since 1967.","Soyuz capsules land on land, not water.","Soyuz was the only way to the ISS from 2011 to 2020."],
fun:"Soyuz crews carry a small soft toy that starts floating when the spacecraft reaches weightlessness, a tradition started by Yuri Gagarin’s doll.",
tl:"Nagdadala ang Soyuz spacecraft ng mga tao sa kalawakan mula pa noong 1967, kaya ito ang pinakamatagal na ginagamit na crewed spacecraft. Ilang taon itong naging tanging paraan upang marating ang International Space Station."});

ADDART({id:"saturnv",t:"Saturn V",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Saturn V",r:["apollo","rocket","skylab","starship","space"],
p:[["The Moon rocket","Saturn V launched the Apollo missions to the Moon."],["A giant","At 110 meters tall, it was taller than the Statue of Liberty."],["Never lost a payload","All 13 Saturn V launches delivered their payloads."]],
l:"Saturn V was the giant three-stage rocket that carried American astronauts to the Moon in the Apollo program. Developed by NASA’s Marshall Space Flight Center under Wernher von Braun, it stood 110.6 meters tall and weighed about 2.9 million kilograms fully fueled. Its first stage, powered by five F-1 engines burning kerosene and liquid oxygen, produced about 35 million newtons of thrust, burning through more than 2 million kilograms of propellant in about two and a half minutes. The second and third stages burned liquid hydrogen. Saturn V first flew uncrewed on Apollo 4 on 9 November 1967; Apollo 8 in December 1968 used it to carry the first humans around the Moon, and Apollo 11 launched on a Saturn V on 16 July 1969. Thirteen Saturn Vs were launched between 1967 and 1973, the last carrying the Skylab space station, and none lost its payload. It remained the most powerful rocket successfully flown until NASA’s Space Launch System in 2022 and SpaceX’s Starship. Three unused Saturn Vs are displayed in Florida, Texas and Alabama.",
s:[["The F-1 engine","The F-1 is still the most powerful single-chamber liquid-fueled rocket engine ever flown. Engineers struggled with violent combustion instability, which they solved by setting off small bombs inside test engines and redesigning the injectors."],["Three stages","The first stage lifted the rocket to about 67 kilometers. The second stage took it nearly to orbit, and the third stage completed the orbit and later fired again to send Apollo toward the Moon. At the top sat the Apollo spacecraft and a launch escape tower."],["Building it","Saturn V was assembled in the Vehicle Assembly Building at Kennedy Space Center, one of the largest buildings in the world by volume, and carried to the launch pad on a giant crawler-transporter moving at about 1.6 kilometers per hour."],["Apollo 6 and Apollo 13","On Apollo 6, an uncrewed test, violent ‘pogo’ vibrations and engine failures nearly ended the flight, but engineers fixed the problems before crews flew. On Apollo 13, the second stage’s center engine shut down early, but the rocket still reached orbit."]],
k:[["Height","110.6 m"],["Fueled weight","About 2.9 million kg"],["First-stage thrust","About 35 million newtons"],["Launches","13 (1967–1973)"],["Chief designer","Wernher von Braun"]],
d:[["1962","Saturn V design chosen"],["1967","First launch, Apollo 4"],["1968","Apollo 8 carries the first crew to the Moon"],["1969","Apollo 11 launches on a Saturn V"],["1973","Final Saturn V launches Skylab"]],
s2:[["Sound of power","The Saturn V launch was so loud that sound waves could be felt kilometers away; NASA placed spectators at least 5 kilometers from the pad. Water was not yet used to suppress sound as on later rockets."],["Von Braun","Wernher von Braun had designed the V-2 missile for Nazi Germany, built using forced labor, before being brought to the United States after World War II. His role in the space program remains admired for its achievements and criticized for his wartime past."]],
sp:["F-1 engines found","In 2013, an expedition funded by Amazon founder Jeff Bezos recovered parts of F-1 engines from Apollo missions from the Atlantic seabed, about 4 kilometers deep, including pieces from Apollo 11."],
g:[["Stage","A section of a rocket with its own engines"],["Thrust","The force that pushes a rocket"],["Combustion instability","Dangerous vibrations inside a rocket engine"],["Crawler-transporter","A giant vehicle that moves rockets to the pad"]],
f:["Saturn V was 110.6 meters tall.","All 13 Saturn Vs delivered their payloads.","The F-1 is the most powerful single-chamber engine flown."],
fun:"Each of the Saturn V’s five F-1 engines pumped about 2.5 metric tons of propellant per second.",
tl:"Inilunsad ng Saturn V ang mga misyong Apollo patungo sa Buwan. Mas mataas ito sa Statue of Liberty sa 110 metro, at lahat ng 13 paglulunsad nito ay nakapaghatid ng karga."});

ADDART({id:"apollo13",t:"Apollo 13",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌘",w:"Apollo 13",r:["apollo","saturnv","moon","fuelcells","artemis"],
p:[["‘A successful failure’","Apollo 13’s Moon landing was cancelled after an explosion, but the crew returned safely."],["‘Houston, we’ve had a problem’","Astronaut Jack Swigert reported the emergency on 13 April 1970."],["A lifeboat in space","The lunar module kept the astronauts alive on the way home."]],
l:"Apollo 13 was the third NASA mission planned to land on the Moon, launched on 11 April 1970 with astronauts Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert and Fred Haise. On 13 April, about 56 hours into the flight and more than 300,000 kilometers from Earth, an oxygen tank in the service module exploded, damaging another tank and crippling the command module’s supply of power, oxygen and water. Swigert radioed, ‘Okay, Houston, we’ve had a problem here.’ The Moon landing was abandoned, and the crew moved into the lunar module, Aquarius, designed to support two people for two days, using it as a lifeboat for four days. With mission control engineers, the crew swung around the Moon and fired the lunar module’s engine to speed their return. They endured cold, little water and rising carbon dioxide, solved by building an adapter from cardboard, plastic bags and tape to fit square air filters into round sockets. On 17 April 1970 they splashed down safely in the Pacific. Apollo 13 set a record for the farthest humans from Earth, which stood until Artemis II in April 2026.",
s:[["The cause","Investigators found that a thermostat switch in oxygen tank 2 had not been upgraded to handle higher voltage, and during ground testing it welded shut, letting heaters overheat and damage wire insulation. In flight, when the crew stirred the tank, a spark ignited the insulation in pure oxygen, causing the explosion."],["The ‘mailbox’","Carbon dioxide built up because the lunar module’s round filter canisters could not handle three people for so long, and the command module’s square canisters did not fit. Engineers on the ground designed a fix from items on board, and the astronauts built it, nicknamed the ‘mailbox’."],["Getting home","Controllers led by flight director Gene Kranz worked out new procedures to power up the cold command module for reentry using limited battery power. The crew separated from the lunar module, nicknamed ‘lifeboat’, shortly before reentry and splashed down near Samoa."],["Records","Swinging around the far side of the Moon, Apollo 13 reached about 400,171 kilometers from Earth, the farthest any humans had traveled. Artemis II broke the record on 6 April 2026, reaching about 406,771 kilometers."]],
k:[["Launch","11 April 1970"],["Crew","Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert, Fred Haise"],["Explosion","13 April 1970"],["Lifeboat","Lunar module Aquarius"],["Splashdown","17 April 1970"]],
d:[["1970","Apollo 13 launches (11 April)"],["1970","Oxygen tank explodes (13 April)"],["1970","Crew splashes down safely (17 April)"],["1995","Film Apollo 13 released"],["2026","Artemis II breaks Apollo 13’s distance record"]],
s2:[["The film","Ron Howard’s 1995 film Apollo 13, starring Tom Hanks as Jim Lovell, made the mission famous to new generations. Its line ‘Houston, we have a problem’ slightly changed the real words."],["Ken Mattingly","Ken Mattingly was replaced on the crew three days before launch because he had been exposed to German measles. He never caught it and helped develop power-up procedures on the ground. He later flew on Apollo 16."]],
sp:["‘Failure is not an option’","The phrase ‘Failure is not an option’, associated with flight director Gene Kranz, was actually written for the 1995 film, though it captured the attitude of mission control. Kranz later used it as the title of his memoir."],
g:[["Service module","The part of Apollo with engines, oxygen and power"],["Command module","The capsule where the crew lived and returned"],["Lunar module","The spacecraft designed to land on the Moon"],["Carbon dioxide","A gas breathed out that is harmful in large amounts"]],
f:["Apollo 13 was called a ‘successful failure’.","The lunar module served as a lifeboat.","Artemis II broke Apollo 13’s distance record in 2026."],
fun:"After splashdown, the lunar module’s maker, Grumman, jokingly sent North American Rockwell, the maker of the damaged service module, an invoice for ‘towing’ the spacecraft back to Earth.",
tl:"Kinansela ang paglapag ng Apollo 13 sa Buwan matapos ang isang pagsabog, ngunit ligtas na nakabalik ang mga astronaut. Nagsilbing lifeboat ang lunar module, at noong 2026 lamang nalampasan ng Artemis II ang rekord nito sa layo mula sa Earth."});

ADDART({id:"spaceanimals",t:"Animals in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🐕",w:"Animals in space",r:["sputnik","space","tardigrades","projectmercury","animaltesting"],
p:[["Pioneers before people","Animals flew into space before humans to test safety."],["Laika","The dog Laika was the first animal to orbit Earth, in 1957."],["Many species","Flies, monkeys, dogs, cats, mice, fish and tardigrades have flown."]],
l:"Animals were sent into space before humans to test whether living things could survive launch, weightlessness and radiation, and they are still used in research. The first animals in space were fruit flies, launched by the United States aboard a captured German V-2 rocket on 20 February 1947; they returned alive. Monkeys followed, beginning with Albert II in 1949, though many early animal flights ended in death. The Soviet Union used stray dogs, believing they were tough. On 3 November 1957, the dog Laika became the first animal to orbit Earth aboard Sputnik 2, but she died within hours from overheating, as there was no plan to return her. On 19 August 1960, the dogs Belka and Strelka orbited Earth and returned safely. In January 1961, the chimpanzee Ham flew on a US Mercury capsule, and in 1963 France launched a cat, Félicette, into space. In 1968, tortoises aboard the Soviet Zond 5 became the first animals to travel around the Moon. Animal flights raised ethical concerns, and many countries honor these animals with monuments.",
s:[["Soviet space dogs","The Soviet Union flew dozens of dogs, mostly female strays weighing about 6 kilograms, on suborbital and orbital flights in the 1950s and 1960s. They were trained in small capsules and centrifuges. Belka and Strelka’s survival paved the way for Yuri Gagarin’s flight in April 1961."],["Laika","Laika, a mixed-breed stray from Moscow, became world-famous. The Soviet government claimed for decades that she lived for days, but in 2002 it was revealed that she died from overheating within hours. A monument to her was unveiled in Moscow in 2008."],["Monkeys and chimpanzees","The United States flew rhesus monkeys and chimpanzees. Ham, trained to pull levers in response to lights, flew on 31 January 1961 and survived. Enos orbited Earth in November 1961, before John Glenn."],["Animals today","Mice, fish, worms, spiders, jellyfish and plants have flown on space stations to study how weightlessness affects bones, muscles, development and behavior. Tardigrades, tiny water bears, survived exposure to open space in 2007."]],
k:[["First animals in space","Fruit flies, 1947"],["First animal in orbit","Laika, 3 November 1957"],["First to orbit and return","Belka and Strelka, 1960"],["First cat","Félicette, 1963"],["First around the Moon","Tortoises on Zond 5, 1968"]],
d:[["1947","Fruit flies reach space and return"],["1949","Albert II monkey reaches space"],["1957","Laika orbits Earth on Sputnik 2"],["1960","Belka and Strelka orbit and return"],["1961","Ham the chimpanzee flies on Mercury"],["1963","Félicette the cat flies"],["1968","Tortoises fly around the Moon"]],
s2:[["A gift from space","One of Strelka’s puppies, Pushinka, was given by Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev to the daughter of US President John F. Kennedy in 1961. Pushinka had puppies with a Kennedy family dog."],["Félicette","Félicette, a stray from Paris, flew to about 157 kilometers on 18 October 1963 and returned alive, but was later euthanized for study. A statue honoring her was unveiled in Strasbourg in 2019 after a crowdfunding campaign."]],
sp:["Tortoises around the Moon","In September 1968, two Russian tortoises aboard Zond 5 flew around the Moon and returned to Earth alive, along with flies and worms, three months before the Apollo 8 astronauts."],
g:[["Suborbital","Reaching space without orbiting"],["Centrifuge","A spinning machine that simulates strong forces"],["Weightlessness","Floating in free fall"],["Euthanize","To end an animal’s life painlessly"]],
f:["Fruit flies were the first animals in space.","Laika was the first animal to orbit Earth.","Tortoises flew around the Moon before humans."],
fun:"Before Apollo 8’s astronauts, the first living things to circle the Moon were two tortoises, which lost some weight but otherwise returned healthy.",
tl:"Lumipad ang mga hayop sa kalawakan bago ang tao upang subukan ang kaligtasan. Ang asong si Laika ang unang hayop na umikot sa Earth noong 1957, at lumipad na rin ang mga langaw, unggoy, aso, pusa, daga, isda at tardigrade."});

ADDART({id:"magnetars",t:"Magnetars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🧲",w:"Magnetar",r:["pulsar","supernova","gammaraybursts","magnet","radioastronomy"],
p:[["The strongest magnets","Magnetars are neutron stars with the strongest magnetic fields known."],["Starquakes","Cracks in their crusts release huge bursts of energy."],["Fast radio bursts","A magnetar was linked to a mysterious fast radio burst in 2020."]],
l:"A magnetar is a type of neutron star, the ultra-dense remnant of a massive star that exploded as a supernova, with an extraordinarily powerful magnetic field, about a thousand times stronger than an ordinary neutron star’s and roughly a quadrillion times stronger than Earth’s. Magnetars are only about 20 kilometers across but contain more mass than the Sun. Their magnetic fields are so intense that they would distort atoms and erase the data on credit cards from halfway to the Moon. As the field twists and decays, it stresses the star’s solid crust, causing ‘starquakes’ that release bursts of X-rays and gamma rays. Magnetars were first identified after a burst of gamma rays on 5 March 1979 was traced to a supernova remnant in the Large Magellanic Cloud. On 27 December 2004, a giant flare from the magnetar SGR 1806-20, about 30,000 light-years away, was so powerful that it briefly affected Earth’s upper atmosphere. About 30 magnetars are known. In 2020, a magnetar in our galaxy produced a fast radio burst, solving part of a long-standing mystery.",
s:[["How magnetars form","When a massive star collapses, its core may become a neutron star. If the core spins very rapidly, perhaps hundreds of times a second, it may generate a magnetic field through a dynamo effect far stronger than usual. Magnetars slow down quickly and stay active for only about 10,000 years."],["Giant flares","Only a few giant flares have been seen. The 2004 flare from SGR 1806-20 released more energy in a tenth of a second than the Sun emits in about 100,000 years, and its gamma rays briefly changed the ionosphere, affecting radio communications."],["Fast radio bursts","Fast radio bursts are extremely brief, powerful flashes of radio waves, mostly from distant galaxies. On 28 April 2020, telescopes detected one from SGR 1935+2154 in the Milky Way at the same time as an X-ray burst, showing that magnetars can produce them."],["Extreme physics","Magnetars’ fields are so strong that, near the surface, light can split into two or merge, and atoms would be stretched into thin needle shapes. Scientists study them to test physics under conditions impossible on Earth."]],
k:[["Type","Neutron star"],["Size","About 20 km across"],["Magnetic field","About a quadrillion times Earth’s"],["Known magnetars","About 30"],["Famous giant flare","SGR 1806-20, 27 December 2004"]],
d:[["1979","Gamma-ray burst from the Large Magellanic Cloud detected"],["1992","Magnetar theory proposed by Robert Duncan and Christopher Thompson"],["1998","Magnetar’s field measured, confirming the theory"],["2004","Giant flare from SGR 1806-20"],["2020","Magnetar produces a fast radio burst"]],
s2:[["Too dangerous to approach","Within about 1,000 kilometers of a magnetar, its magnetic field would kill a person by distorting the atoms in their body. Fortunately, the nearest known magnetars are thousands of light-years away."],["The 1979 event","The burst of 5 March 1979 was detected by nine spacecraft across the solar system, allowing astronomers to pinpoint its source in the remnant N49. It was far brighter than any burst seen before."]],
sp:["Duncan and Thompson","Astrophysicists Robert Duncan and Christopher Thompson proposed magnetars in 1992 to explain unusual repeating gamma-ray bursts. Their idea was confirmed in 1998 by measurements of a magnetar’s slowing spin."],
g:[["Neutron star","The collapsed core of a massive star"],["Starquake","A sudden shift in a neutron star’s crust"],["Fast radio burst","A millisecond flash of radio waves"],["Ionosphere","A charged layer of Earth’s upper atmosphere"]],
f:["Magnetars have the strongest magnetic fields known.","A magnetar flare affected Earth’s atmosphere in 2004.","A magnetar produced a fast radio burst in 2020."],
fun:"A teaspoon of material from a neutron star like a magnetar would weigh about a billion metric tons on Earth.",
tl:"Ang magnetar ay neutron star na may pinakamalakas na magnetic field na kilala. Naglalabas ng malalaking pagsabog ng enerhiya ang mga bitak sa balat nito, at iniugnay ang isang magnetar sa isang fast radio burst noong 2020."});

ADDART({id:"lensing",t:"Gravitational Lensing",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔍",w:"Gravitational lens",r:["relat","einstein","darkmatter","jwst","eclipse"],
p:[["Gravity bends light","Massive objects bend light passing near them, like a lens."],["Proof for Einstein","The 1919 eclipse confirmed that the Sun bends starlight."],["A cosmic magnifying glass","Lensing reveals distant galaxies, dark matter and planets."]],
l:"Gravitational lensing is the bending of light by the gravity of a massive object, such as a galaxy, a cluster of galaxies, a star or a black hole, which acts like a lens. According to Albert Einstein’s general theory of relativity, published in 1915, mass curves space-time, and light follows that curve. Einstein predicted that the Sun would bend starlight by a small amount; British astronomer Arthur Eddington’s expedition to observe the total solar eclipse of 29 May 1919 measured this effect, making Einstein world-famous. Lensing can produce multiple images of the same distant object, arcs and complete circles called Einstein rings. The first lensed object, the ‘Twin Quasar’, was discovered in 1979. Strong lensing by galaxy clusters magnifies faint galaxies behind them, letting telescopes such as the James Webb Space Telescope see some of the earliest galaxies. Weak lensing, slight distortions of the shapes of many galaxies, maps invisible dark matter. Microlensing, the brightening of a star when another passes in front of it, has revealed planets and isolated black holes.",
s:[["Types of lensing","In strong lensing, a massive galaxy or cluster creates obvious multiple images, arcs or rings. In weak lensing, distortions are tiny and must be measured statistically over millions of galaxies. In microlensing, the lens is a star or planet, causing a temporary brightening rather than visible distortion."],["The 1919 eclipse","During a total eclipse, stars near the Sun become visible. Eddington and colleagues photographed stars from Príncipe, an island off Africa, and Sobral, Brazil. The stars appeared shifted by an amount matching Einstein’s prediction, twice the amount predicted by Newtonian physics."],["Mapping dark matter","Because lensing depends on all mass, including invisible dark matter, astronomers use it to map dark matter around galaxy clusters. ESA’s Euclid telescope, launched in 2023, is mapping weak lensing across a third of the sky to study dark matter and dark energy."],["Magnified views","JWST’s first deep field image in July 2022 showed the galaxy cluster SMACS 0723 bending light from galaxies far behind it into arcs. Lensing has magnified individual stars billions of light-years away, such as Earendel, seen as it was about 900 million years after the Big Bang."]],
k:[["Cause","Gravity bending light"],["Predicted by","Albert Einstein (general relativity, 1915)"],["First confirmed","1919 solar eclipse"],["First lensed quasar","Twin Quasar, 1979"],["Uses","Distant galaxies, dark matter, planets"]],
d:[["1915","Einstein publishes general relativity"],["1919","Eclipse confirms light bending"],["1936","Einstein publishes on lensing by stars"],["1979","Twin Quasar discovered"],["2022","JWST’s first deep field shows lensed galaxies"],["2023","Euclid launches to map lensing"]],
s2:[["Einstein rings and crosses","When a source, lens and observer line up exactly, the source appears as a ring around the lens, an Einstein ring. The ‘Einstein Cross’ is a quasar seen as four images around a foreground galaxy."],["Seeing a supernova again","In 2014, a supernova behind a galaxy cluster appeared as four images. Astronomers predicted another image would appear about a year later because its light took a longer path, and in 2015 it did, as forecast."]],
sp:["Planets by microlensing","Microlensing can detect planets far from their stars and even free-floating planets with no star. NASA’s Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, planned for launch in the late 2020s, will search for thousands of planets this way."],
g:[["Space-time","Space and time combined in relativity"],["Einstein ring","A ring-shaped image produced by lensing"],["Quasar","A very bright galaxy center powered by a black hole"],["Microlensing","Brightening caused by a star or planet acting as a lens"]],
f:["Gravity can bend light.","The 1919 eclipse confirmed Einstein’s prediction.","Lensing helps map invisible dark matter."],
fun:"Astronomers correctly predicted the date when a lensed supernova would reappear in a new spot in the sky, because its light took a longer path around a galaxy.",
tl:"Ang gravitational lensing ay pagbaluktot ng liwanag dahil sa grabidad ng malaking bagay, na parang lente. Kinumpirma ng eklipse noong 1919 ang hula ni Einstein, at ibinubunyag ng lensing ang malalayong galaxy, dark matter at mga planeta."});

ADDART({id:"interstellarobjects",t:"Interstellar Objects",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌠",w:"Interstellar object",r:["comet","asteroidbelt","exoplanet","scope","fermiparadox"],
p:[["Visitors from other stars","Interstellar objects come from outside our solar system."],["Only three known","‘Oumuamua, Borisov and 3I/ATLAS have been found so far."],["Clues from far away","They carry material from other planetary systems."]],
l:"Interstellar objects are asteroids, comets or other bodies that originate outside the solar system and pass through it on their way between the stars. Astronomers recognize them by their speed and hyperbolic orbits, open paths showing that they are not bound to the Sun. Scientists had long expected them, because planetary systems throw out huge numbers of small bodies as they form, but none was identified until 19 October 2017, when the Pan-STARRS telescope in Hawaii discovered 1I/ʻOumuamua, a strange, elongated or flattened object that tumbled through space without a visible tail and was gently pushed by a force not fully explained. In August 2019, amateur astronomer Gennadiy Borisov found 2I/Borisov, which looked like an ordinary comet but was unusually rich in carbon monoxide. On 1 July 2025, the ATLAS survey telescope in Chile discovered 3I/ATLAS, a large, active comet that may be billions of years older than the solar system. Interstellar objects offer samples of other planetary systems. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory is expected to find many more.",
s:[["ʻOumuamua","ʻOumuamua, a Hawaiian name meaning roughly ‘scout’ or ‘messenger from afar arriving first’, was spotted after it had already passed the Sun. It was dark reddish, perhaps 100 to 400 meters long, and accelerated slightly as it left. Most scientists think gas released from ices, such as hydrogen, caused the push, though one astronomer, Avi Loeb, suggested it might be alien technology, an idea most researchers rejected."],["2I/Borisov","Borisov was found by Gennadiy Borisov using a telescope he built himself in Crimea. It had a fuzzy coma and tail like a typical comet, showing that comets in other planetary systems can be similar to ours, though its high carbon monoxide content suggests it formed in a very cold place."],["3I/ATLAS","3I/ATLAS traveled at about 58 kilometers per second relative to the Sun and came closest to the Sun in late October 2025. Spacecraft at Mars and elsewhere observed it, finding a coma rich in carbon dioxide, nickel and water. Its path suggests it comes from the older ‘thick disk’ of the Milky Way."],["Chasing a visitor","Because interstellar objects move fast and are found late, catching one is difficult. ESA’s Comet Interceptor mission, planned for launch around 2029, will wait in space for a suitable comet, possibly an interstellar one, to visit."]],
k:[["Known objects","Three (as of 2026)"],["First","1I/ʻOumuamua, 2017"],["Second","2I/Borisov, 2019"],["Third","3I/ATLAS, 2025"],["How identified","Hyperbolic orbit and high speed"]],
d:[["2017","ʻOumuamua discovered (19 October)"],["2019","2I/Borisov discovered (30 August)"],["2025","3I/ATLAS discovered (1 July)"],["2025","3I/ATLAS passes closest to the Sun (October)"],["c. 2029","Comet Interceptor planned launch"]],
s2:[["Naming","Interstellar objects receive the letter I and a number in order of discovery: 1I, 2I, 3I. The International Astronomical Union created the category in 2017 for ʻOumuamua."],["How many?","Astronomers estimate that at any time, several interstellar objects the size of ʻOumuamua may be passing within the orbit of Neptune, but most are too faint to detect with current telescopes."]],
sp:["Interstellar meteors?","Some scientists have claimed that small meteors that struck Earth, such as one over Papua New Guinea in 2014, came from interstellar space, but these claims are disputed because of uncertainties in the data."],
g:[["Interstellar","Between the stars"],["Hyperbolic orbit","An open path not bound to the Sun"],["Coma","The cloud of gas and dust around a comet"],["Survey telescope","A telescope that scans large areas of sky"]],
f:["Only three interstellar objects are known.","ʻOumuamua was the first, found in 2017.","3I/ATLAS may be older than the solar system."],
fun:"Gennadiy Borisov discovered the second interstellar object with a 0.65-meter telescope he built himself.",
tl:"Ang interstellar object ay mga bagay na nagmumula sa labas ng ating solar system. Tatlo pa lamang ang kilala, ang ʻOumuamua, Borisov at 3I/ATLAS, at nagdadala ang mga ito ng materyal mula sa ibang sistema ng planeta."});

ADDART({id:"spacebody",t:"The Human Body in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🧑‍🚀",w:"Effect of spaceflight on the human body",r:["astronauts","iss","bones","muscles","cosmicrays"],
p:[["Weightlessness changes the body","In space, bones and muscles weaken and fluids shift upward."],["Radiation risk","Astronauts are exposed to more radiation than on Earth."],["Exercise every day","Astronauts exercise about two hours a day to stay healthy."]],
l:"Spaceflight affects almost every system of the human body, a key challenge for long missions to the Moon and Mars. In weightlessness, without gravity pulling down, body fluids shift toward the head, giving astronauts puffy faces and ‘bird legs’ and affecting their vision. Without weight to support, bones lose about 1 to 1.5 percent of their mass per month in some areas, and muscles weaken; astronauts exercise about two hours every day on treadmills, bicycles and resistance machines. The spine lengthens, making astronauts up to about 5 centimeters taller temporarily. Many feel ‘space sickness’ in the first days as the balance organs in the inner ear adjust. Radiation from the Sun and cosmic rays is much higher than on Earth, increasing long-term cancer risk. Isolation and confinement can affect mood and sleep. NASA’s Twins Study, comparing astronaut Scott Kelly, who spent nearly a year on the ISS in 2015–2016, with his identical twin, Mark, found many changes, most of which returned to normal after landing. The longest single spaceflight was Valeri Polyakov’s 437 days on Mir in 1994–1995.",
s:[["Bones and muscles","Weight-bearing bones such as the hips and spine lose minerals fastest. Without countermeasures, astronauts could lose bone at about ten times the rate of osteoporosis on Earth. Exercise and good nutrition reduce but do not completely prevent losses, and bones may take years to recover."],["Eyes and brain","About 70 percent of astronauts on long missions develop changes in their eyes, such as swelling of the optic nerve and flattening of the eyeball, called spaceflight-associated neuro-ocular syndrome. The brain also shifts slightly upward in the skull."],["Radiation","On the ISS, astronauts receive about 10 times or more the radiation dose people get on Earth in the same time. Beyond Earth’s magnetic field, on trips to Mars, doses would be much higher. Shielding, shorter travel times and medicines are being studied."],["Coming home","After returning, astronauts often feel dizzy and heavy, may struggle to walk and need weeks or months to readapt. Some reported that they tried to let go of objects in midair, forgetting that they would fall."]],
k:[["Bone loss","About 1–1.5% per month in some bones"],["Daily exercise","About 2 hours"],["Height gain","Up to about 5 cm"],["Longest single flight","Valeri Polyakov, 437 days"],["Twins Study","Scott and Mark Kelly"]],
d:[["1961","Gagarin shows humans can survive in space"],["1973–1974","Skylab studies long stays in space"],["1995","Polyakov completes 437 days on Mir"],["2016","Scott Kelly returns from nearly a year in space"],["2019","NASA Twins Study results published"],["2023","Frank Rubio sets a US record of 371 days"]],
s2:[["Space and the immune system","Spaceflight can alter the immune system, and dormant viruses such as those causing cold sores sometimes reactivate in astronauts. Scientists also study how microbes behave aboard spacecraft."],["Artificial gravity","Future spacecraft might spin to create artificial gravity, reducing bone and muscle loss. Experiments with short-arm centrifuges, which spin people lying down, are being tested on Earth."]],
sp:["Sleeping in space","Astronauts sleep in small cabins, zipped into sleeping bags attached to walls so they do not float around. Without a sense of up or down, they can sleep in any orientation, and some report vivid dreams."],
g:[["Weightlessness","Floating in free fall"],["Countermeasure","An action to prevent harm"],["Space sickness","Nausea in the first days in space"],["Osteoporosis","A disease that weakens bones"]],
f:["Astronauts grow up to about 5 cm taller in space.","Astronauts exercise about two hours a day.","The longest spaceflight lasted 437 days."],
fun:"Astronauts’ feet lose their tough skin in space because they rarely walk, while the tops of their feet get calluses from hooking under handrails.",
tl:"Binabago ng kalawakan ang katawan: humihina ang buto at kalamnan at umaakyat ang mga likido sa ulo. Mas mataas ang radyasyon doon, kaya nag-eehersisyo ang mga astronaut nang mga dalawang oras araw-araw."});

;
